const DATA = [{"id": "china-rare-earth-export-controls", "title": "China's rare-earth chokehold", "region": "China (and US, EU, Japan as importers)", "years": "2025–2026", "subtopic": "4.3 (also 4.1)", "concepts": ["national security argument", "export restrictions", "risks of specialisation", "production subsidy", "price floor", "retaliation"], "hook": "China refines about 90% of the world's rare earths and makes about 94% of its magnets, so a licensing rule from Beijing could halt car factories on other continents.", "facts": ["IEA: China did about 91% of the world's rare-earth refining and made about 94% of its magnets in 2024, while mining only about 60% of the raw material.", "April 2025: China required export licences for 7 rare earths and for magnets. Magnet exports fell 74% year on year in May 2025, and carmakers in Europe warned of production stoppages.", "October 2025: China announced a wider rule covering foreign-made goods containing Chinese rare earths, then suspended it until November 2026.", "Earlier warning: in 2010 China halted rare-earth shipments to Japan during a territorial dispute. Japan responded by funding the Australian miner Lynas and cut its reliance on China from about 90% to about 60%.", "IEA (2026): projects outside China will meet only about a quarter of refining demand by 2035."], "diagram": "World market for rare earths: export licences shift supply left and raise the world price. A subsidy diagram shows importers paying to build their own producers.", "evaluation": ["National security works both ways: China uses export controls as leverage, and importers such as Japan and the EU use that to justify subsidising their own supply. A strong case that the national security argument is not just a pretext.", "Limits of comparative advantage: specialisation lowered costs for decades but created a single point of failure. An evaluative point against pure free-trade reasoning.", "Cost of diversifying: building supply outside China needs subsidies and takes 10+ years, as Japan's Lynas investment shows. That is a misallocation of resources if the threat fades.", "Contested effectiveness: China's total rare-earth export volume in 2025 was still high, so the controls were targeted rather than a blanket ban."], "rating": {"relevance": 4, "data": 4, "recency": 5, "evaluation": 5}, "verdict": "Excellent for national security and the risks of specialisation, with a ready-made subsidy example. Weakness: export controls are not one of the IB's listed protection tools, so frame it as a 4.3 argument.", "sources": ["https://aa.com.tr/en/economy/international-energy-agency-warns-rare-earth-supply-diversification-lags-rising-global-demand/3898514", "https://www.whitecase.com/insight-alert/china-imposes-extraterritorial-jurisdiction-and-50-rule-export-controls-rare-earth", "https://investors.mpmaterials.com/investor-news/news-details/2025/MP-Materials-Announces-Transformational-Public-Private-Partnership-with-the-Department-of-Defense-to-Accelerate-U-S--Rare-Earth-Magnet-Independence/default.aspx"], "mem": {"tag": "Rare-earth chokehold", "story": "China refines about 91% → licences rare-earth exports (2025) → magnet exports fall 74% → importers rush to build their own supply", "numbers": [["91%", "China's share of world rare-earth refining (2024)"], ["94%", "China's share of world magnet production"], ["74%", "fall in China's magnet exports, May 2025"]], "use": "national security argument, risks of specialisation", "but": "Diversifying is slow and costly: Japan needed about a decade to cut its reliance on China from about 90% to about 60%."}}, {"id": "eu-countervailing-duties-chinese-evs", "title": "Europe's tariff wall vs. BYD", "region": "European Union & China", "years": "2024–2026", "subtopic": "4.2, 4.3", "concepts": ["countervailing (anti-subsidy) duty", "production subsidy", "anti-dumping", "retaliation", "tariff-jumping FDI", "minimum import price"], "hook": "The EU put tariffs of up to 45% on Chinese EVs to offset Beijing's subsidies, and China answered with duties on cognac, pork and cheese.", "facts": ["Definitive countervailing duties from Oct 2024 for 5 years: BYD 17.0%, Geely 18.8%, SAIC 35.3%, on top of the 10% standard tariff (total up to 45.3%). Tesla Shanghai got 7.8%.", "Chinese brands' share of the European car market doubled to 5.9% in May 2025 from 2.9% a year earlier (JATO). Much of the growth came from plug-in hybrids, which the duties do not cover.", "China's retaliation: brandy duties up to 34.9% (from Jul 2025), final pork anti-dumping duties of 4.9–19.8% (Dec 2025, down from provisional rates up to 62.4%), and duties on EU dairy.", "Jan 2026: the Commission set out minimum-price undertakings as an alternative to the duties. The first was accepted in Feb 2026, for VW Anhui's Cupra Tavascan.", "BYD started trial production at its plant in Szeged, Hungary in early 2026, with a design capacity of about 200,000 cars a year, so it can sell inside the EU tariff-free."], "diagram": "Tariff diagram (EU car market); subsidy diagram (Chinese production subsidy lowering the export price)", "evaluation": ["Stakeholders: EU carmakers and workers gain. EU consumers face higher EV prices, which slows decarbonisation. German carmakers with big China exposure opposed the duties for fear of retaliation.", "Legitimacy: countervailing duties are WTO-permitted responses to subsidies, unlike pure protection. But it is debatable whether China's cost advantage comes from subsidies or from genuine economies of scale and comparative advantage.", "Unintended consequences: Chinese firms switched to plug-in hybrids and invested in EU factories (tariff-jumping FDI), so the duties shifted where production happens rather than stopping it.", "Retaliation hit unrelated sectors such as French cognac, Spanish pork and Irish and Dutch dairy. Countries that did not benefit from the EV duties paid for them."], "rating": {"relevance": 5, "data": 5, "recency": 5, "evaluation": 5}, "verdict": "The best example of anti-subsidy protection and retaliation. Weakness: the status of minimum-price deals for BYD, Geely and SAIC is unclear, so cite the 2024 rates as the confirmed measure.", "sources": ["https://www.automotiveworld.com/news-releases/jato-chinese-automakers-double-european-market-share-in-may/", "https://www.euronews.com/2025/12/16/china-puts-anti-dumping-tariff-of-up-to-189-on-pork-imports-from-eu", "https://cnevpost.com/2026/01/12/china-eu-reach-consensus-price-undertakings-ev-exports/"], "mem": {"tag": "EV tariff wall", "story": "China subsidises EVs → EU adds duties, up to 45.3% in total → China hits back on brandy, pork and dairy → BYD builds a factory in Hungary", "numbers": [["45.3%", "highest total EU tariff on a Chinese EV maker (SAIC)"], ["17%", "BYD's extra duty, the lowest of the big three"], ["5.9%", "Chinese brands' share of Europe's car market, May 2025"]], "use": "countervailing duties, retaliation, tariff-jumping FDI", "but": "Chinese firms got round it: plug-in hybrids aren't covered, and BYD now builds cars inside the EU."}}, {"id": "eu-steel-quota-2026", "title": "Fortress Europe for steel", "region": "European Union (vs. China, India, Turkey, others)", "years": "2025–2026", "subtopic": "4.2, 4.3", "concepts": ["import quota", "tariff-rate quota", "trade deflection", "protecting jobs", "dumping / overcapacity", "retaliation spillover"], "hook": "With the world's surplus steel heading for Europe, the EU cut its duty-free import quota by almost half and doubled the tariff above it.", "facts": ["When other markets raised steel tariffs in 2025, surplus steel was diverted towards Europe, a process called trade deflection.", "EU steel imports rose 14% in 2025, while EU steel output fell to a record low of 125.8 Mt (Eurofer). The OECD puts global steel overcapacity at about 650 Mt.", "A new EU regulation from 1 Jul 2026 replaced the expiring safeguard. The duty-free quota was cut about 47% to 18.3 Mt a year, and the out-of-quota tariff rose from 25% to 50%.", "Eurofer claims up to 80,000 jobs were safeguarded across the value chain (an industry estimate). In 2024, more than 18,000 job cuts had been announced."], "diagram": "Quota diagram (tariff-rate quota: quota quantity at world price, 50% tariff beyond it; quota rents to licence holders)", "evaluation": ["Protecting jobs: steel-making jobs may be saved, but steel-using industries (cars, machinery, construction) employ far more people and lose from higher input prices.", "Trade deflection is a domino effect: one country's protection diverts surplus to others and spreads protection. A good chain-of-reasoning point.", "Quota vs tariff: a quota caps quantity regardless of price, so domestic prices can rise without limit when demand is strong. Quota rents go to whoever holds the import licences, not the government.", "Root cause: the underlying problem is global overcapacity, mainly subsidised Chinese capacity, which is arguably better tackled multilaterally. The WTO's weakness pushes countries toward unilateral measures."], "rating": {"relevance": 5, "data": 4, "recency": 5, "evaluation": 4}, "verdict": "The best recent quota example, and it shows trade deflection in action. Weakness: job figures come mostly from the industry lobby (Eurofer), so present them as contested.", "sources": ["https://www.crowell.com/en/insights/client-alerts/eu-steel-overcapacity-regulation-new-permanent-measure-in-force-from-1-july-2026", "https://www.eurofer.eu/eu-trade-measure-saves-steel-industry-from-cliff-edge-as-record-imports-surge", "https://econofact.org/steel-tariffs-and-u-s-jobs-revisited"], "mem": {"tag": "Fortress Europe steel", "story": "World steel glut → cheap steel floods into the EU → duty-free quota cut 47% → 50% tariff on anything above it", "numbers": [["18.3", "million tonnes of duty-free steel imports allowed per year"], ["47%", "cut in the EU's duty-free steel quota"], ["50%", "EU tariff on steel imports above the quota"]], "use": "import quota, tariff-rate quota, protecting jobs", "but": "Car and machinery makers pay more for steel, and they employ far more people than steel mills."}}, {"id": "australia-china-barley-wine", "title": "Barley, wine and a diplomatic freeze", "region": "China & Australia", "years": "2020–2024", "subtopic": "4.3, 4.4", "concepts": ["anti-dumping duties", "retaliation / political protection", "trade diversion", "WTO dispute settlement", "export dependence"], "hook": "China used anti-dumping duties of 80.5% on barley and up to 218% on wine to punish Australia politically, and lifted them once relations thawed.", "facts": ["May 2020: China put 80.5% anti-dumping and anti-subsidy duties on Australian barley, a trade worth about A$916m in 2018–19. This followed Australia's call for an inquiry into COVID's origins.", "Wine duties of 107.1–212.1% (Nov 2020), raised to 116.2–218.4% (Mar 2021), on a trade worth over A$1bn a year. China had been Australian wine's most valuable market.", "Australia took both cases to the WTO. China lifted the barley duties in Aug 2023 and the wine duties on 29 Mar 2024, and Australia dropped its WTO cases.", "Recovery: Australian wine exports rose 34% to A$2.55bn in 2024. By early 2025, wine exports to China were back to about A$1.03bn a year, but volume was about 44% below the 2018 peak."], "diagram": "Tariff diagram (China's wine or barley market); optionally a demand shift for Australian exporters", "evaluation": ["Anti-dumping used as cover: the timing points to political retaliation, which shows how WTO-legal tools can be misused.", "Australian farmers diverted barley to other markets, so losses were smaller than feared. Wine had fewer substitute markets and suffered more, which shows that the elasticity of export markets matters.", "Chinese consumers and brewers also lost: China cited a shortage of domestic barley when it lifted the duties.", "The disputes were settled through diplomacy, not rulings. This shows both the value of the WTO process as leverage and its limits."], "rating": {"relevance": 5, "data": 5, "recency": 4, "evaluation": 5}, "verdict": "A strong, complete story from imposition through recovery, ideal for retaliation and anti-dumping misuse. Weakness: it ended in 2024, so it is not the newest.", "sources": ["https://hongkongfp.com/2024/03/28/china-lifts-punitive-tariffs-on-australian-wine-as-trade-ties-improve/", "https://www.trademinister.gov.au/minister/don-farrell/media-release/first-fields-barley-make-their-way-china", "https://www.just-drinks.com/news/australian-wine-export-value-bounces-back-in-china-volumes-not-yet-recovered"], "mem": {"tag": "Barley and wine freeze", "story": "Australia calls for a COVID inquiry → China puts 80.5% duties on barley and up to 218% on wine → Australia goes to the WTO → duties lifted 2023–24", "numbers": [["80.5%", "China's duty on Australian barley (2020)"], ["218%", "top Chinese duty on Australian wine"], ["34%", "rise in Australian wine exports in 2024 after the duties ended"]], "use": "retaliation, anti-dumping misuse, export dependence", "but": "Barley farmers found new buyers fast but wine makers couldn't, so the damage depended on how easily exports could be redirected."}}, {"id": "indonesia-nickel-export-ban", "title": "Indonesia's nickel gamble", "region": "Indonesia (vs. EU at WTO)", "years": "2020–2026", "subtopic": "4.3, 4.4, 4.10", "concepts": ["infant industry argument", "export ban", "moving up the value chain", "WTO dispute", "appeal into the void", "overcapacity", "resource-based industrialisation"], "hook": "Indonesia banned exports of raw nickel ore, lost at the WTO, appealed to a court that no longer works, and grew its nickel exports tenfold.", "facts": ["Jan 2020: ban on nickel ore exports to force domestic smelting and battery production ('downstreaming').", "Nickel product exports grew from about $3.3bn (2017–18) to $33.5bn (2023) and $33.6bn (2024).", "Indonesia supplied roughly 58–64% of global nickel in 2024 (sources differ), up from a small share a decade earlier.", "WTO panel ruling (DS592, Nov 2022) found the ban broke GATT rules. Indonesia appealed in Dec 2022 to the non-functioning Appellate Body, so the ruling cannot be enforced.", "In 2026 Indonesia cut its ore mining quota to about 260–270 million tonnes (from 379 million in 2025) to prop up prices, after its own flood of supply had pushed world nickel prices down."], "diagram": "No standard diagram. In supply and demand terms, the ban lowers the domestic ore price, which acts as an implicit subsidy to domestic smelters", "evaluation": ["Infant industry success? Export value rose tenfold and processing capacity was built. This is one of the strongest modern cases for the argument, though much of the investment was foreign (largely Chinese).", "Costs: overcapacity pushed world nickel prices down, creating a profitability crisis. The industry relies heavily on coal-fired power, raising environmental concerns that may limit access to Western markets.", "Fairness: developing countries argue that rich countries industrialised the same way. Developed countries see it as an export restriction that breaks WTO rules.", "Institutional point: the 'appeal into the void' shows how the Appellate Body's collapse lets countries ignore rulings."], "rating": {"relevance": 5, "data": 4, "recency": 4, "evaluation": 5}, "verdict": "The best real infant-industry case, and it links to the WTO crisis. Weakness: an export ban is not one of the standard IB protection diagrams, so the analysis has to be built verbally.", "sources": ["https://en.antaranews.com/news/320483/downstreaming-propels-nickel-export-value-tenfold-minister", "https://www.csis.org/blogs/charting-geoeconomics/indonesian-industrialization-downstreaming-value-chain", "https://www.mining.com/web/indonesia-appeals-wto-ruling-on-nickel-ore-export-ban-dispute/"], "mem": {"tag": "Nickel ore ban", "story": "Ban raw nickel ore exports (2020) → smelters built at home → nickel exports ×10 → WTO rules it illegal → Indonesia appeals 'into the void'", "numbers": [["$3.3bn", "Indonesia's nickel exports before the ban (2017–18)"], ["$33.6bn", "nickel exports in 2024"], ["60%", "Indonesia's approximate share of world nickel supply (2024)"]], "use": "infant industry argument, export ban, WTO disputes", "but": "Much of the industry is Chinese-owned and coal-powered, and the flood of supply crashed world nickel prices."}}, {"id": "vietnam-china-plus-one", "title": "Vietnam: China plus one", "region": "Vietnam and China", "years": "2018–2026", "subtopic": "4.1", "concepts": ["comparative advantage", "specialisation", "economies of scale", "global value chains", "trade diversion", "transshipment"], "hook": "When firms moved factories out of China, many went to Vietnam, which now exports about $475bn a year, much of it built from a record $186bn of Chinese parts.", "facts": ["Vietnam's exports rose about 17% in 2025 to about $475bn, and growth was about 8%.", "Imports from China hit a record $186bn in 2025 (2024: $144.2bn): Vietnam assembles Chinese components into finished goods.", "Specialisation: phone and component exports were $52.3bn in 2023, mostly from Samsung. Vietnam makes about 13% of the world's smartphones.", "'China plus one': firms keep China but add a second, cheaper base to cut labour costs and avoid tariffs aimed at Chinese goods."], "diagram": "PPC and comparative advantage diagram (HL: opportunity cost calculation)", "evaluation": ["Comparative advantage in labour-intensive assembly plus FDI-driven economies of scale shows the benefits of trade for a developing economy.", "Trade diversion: tariffs aimed at China mostly moved assembly to Vietnam rather than bringing it home, so buyers still import, just from a different and slightly higher-cost source.", "Value-added question: if much of the value is Chinese parts and Korean ownership, Vietnam's domestic gain from specialisation is smaller than gross export figures suggest.", "Risk: relying on a few export markets and one firm (Samsung) leaves Vietnam exposed to demand shocks and trade disputes."], "rating": {"relevance": 4, "data": 5, "recency": 5, "evaluation": 4}, "verdict": "The best 4.1 example linking comparative advantage and economies of scale to current events. Weakness: separating genuine comparative advantage from transshipment is contested.", "sources": ["https://www.ajot.com/news/vietnams-annual-growth-reaches-8-as-trade-surplus-with-us-hits-record-despite-tariffs", "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2025-07-04/china-s-share-of-us-imports-falls-to-7-as-vietnam-closes-in", "https://eastandpartners.com/news/chinese-share-of-us-imports-halves-to-9-dhl/"], "mem": {"tag": "China plus one", "story": "Firms move assembly out of China → Vietnam assembles Chinese parts → exports reach $475bn → growth about 8%", "numbers": [["$475bn", "Vietnam's exports in 2025"], ["$186bn", "Vietnam's imports from China in 2025, mostly parts"], ["13%", "Vietnam's share of world smartphone production"]], "use": "comparative advantage, specialisation, economies of scale", "but": "Much of the value comes from Chinese parts and Korean firms, so Vietnam's own gain is smaller than the export total suggests."}}, {"id": "eu-mercosur-agreement", "title": "EU–Mercosur: 25 years to a deal", "region": "EU & Mercosur (Brazil, Argentina, Uruguay, Paraguay)", "years": "1999–2026", "subtopic": "4.4", "concepts": ["free trade agreement", "customs union (Mercosur)", "trading blocs", "trade creation / diversion", "safeguards", "agricultural protection"], "hook": "After more than 25 years of talks, two trading blocs covering over 700 million people created a free trade area, while French and Belgian farmers protested with tractors in the streets.", "facts": ["Signed 17 Jan 2026, creating a free trade zone of more than 700 million people. It removes duties on about 91% of EU exports to Mercosur, phased in over up to 15 years for sensitive goods.", "Mercosur beef gets a 99,000-tonne quota at a 7.5% in-quota tariff, a tariff-rate quota that protects EU farmers.", "Jan 2026: the European Parliament referred the deal to the EU Court of Justice. Despite this, the interim trade agreement has applied provisionally since 1 May 2026.", "Feb 2026: Parliament approved an agricultural safeguard regulation that allows preferences to be suspended if imports surge.", "Sep 2026: the EU court refused Poland's request to suspend the deal. The main legal case is still pending, so the deal could still be halted."], "diagram": "None standard. Explain trade creation and diversion in words; tariff removal can be shown as a reverse tariff diagram for EU car exports to Mercosur", "evaluation": ["Winners: EU exporters of cars, machinery and wine, and Mercosur farm exporters. Losers: EU beef and poultry farmers, and Mercosur manufacturers facing EU competition.", "Trade creation vs diversion: cheaper Mercosur beef may displace more efficient non-member suppliers (diversion) as well as higher-cost EU producers (creation).", "Environmental concerns (Amazon deforestation, differing standards) are a non-economic argument that slowed the deal for years.", "Geopolitics: Germany and Spain pushed for the deal to diversify trade as protectionism rose elsewhere, which shows integration can be a response to other countries' tariffs."], "rating": {"relevance": 5, "data": 4, "recency": 5, "evaluation": 5}, "verdict": "The clearest current bloc-to-bloc trade deal, with built-in stakeholder conflict. Weakness: its legal status is unresolved, so check for court rulings before the exam.", "sources": ["https://www.euronews.com/2026/05/01/contentious-eu-mercosur-trade-deal-takes-provisional-effect", "https://www.europarl.europa.eu/news/en/press-room/20260205IPR33618/mercosur-parliament-approves-safeguard-clauses-to-protect-eu-agriculture", "https://curia.europa.eu/site/upload/docs/application/pdf/2026-09/cp260135en.pdf"], "mem": {"tag": "EU–Mercosur deal", "story": "25 years of talks → signed January 2026 → duties cut on 91% of EU exports → farmers protest → EU court challenge", "numbers": [["700", "million people in the new free trade area"], ["91%", "share of EU exports to Mercosur that become duty-free"], ["99,000", "tonnes of Mercosur beef allowed in at a low tariff"]], "use": "free trade agreements, trading blocs, trade creation and diversion", "but": "EU beef farmers lose, and the EU court could still halt the deal."}}, {"id": "afcfta", "title": "AfCFTA: a continental market on paper", "region": "Africa (54 signatories)", "years": "2018–2026", "subtopic": "4.4", "concepts": ["free trade area", "regional trading bloc", "non-tariff barriers", "rules of origin", "trade facilitation", "economies of scale"], "hook": "Africa signed the world's largest free trade area by number of countries, yet only about 15% of African trade is with other African countries.", "facts": ["54 of 55 African Union members have signed (all except Eritrea), and about 49–50 have ratified. Trading under the agreement began on 1 Jan 2021.", "Intra-African trade was about $213.8bn in 2025, roughly 15–18% of Africa's total trade. Afreximbank forecasts about $230bn in 2026.", "Only about 24–25 states had published their tariff schedules, and only 17 had full schedules.", "Rules of origin had been agreed for 92.43% of tariff lines by Jul 2025, with textiles and cars the last disputed sectors.", "World Bank (2020) projected that full implementation could raise income by 7% (about $450bn) and lift 30 million people out of extreme poverty by 2035, mostly through trade facilitation rather than tariff cuts."], "diagram": "None", "evaluation": ["Potential gains: a larger market lets firms reach economies of scale and diversify away from raw-commodity exports to the EU and China.", "The binding constraint is non-tariff: poor infrastructure, customs delays and rules-of-origin paperwork, which is why the World Bank attributes most gains to trade facilitation.", "Uneven gains: more industrialised members (South Africa, Egypt, Kenya, Morocco) may gain more, and the least developed countries fear losing tariff revenue and infant industries.", "Implementation gap: signing and ratifying does not mean trade actually flows. A good example of integration on paper versus in practice."], "rating": {"relevance": 5, "data": 3, "recency": 5, "evaluation": 4}, "verdict": "A strong developing-world bloc example and good for evaluating the limits of integration. Weakness: the data is patchy and inconsistent, and projections date from 2020.", "sources": ["https://www.riotimesonline.com/afcfta-status-2026", "https://www.ecofinagency.com/news/1104-54591-intra-african-trade-set-to-grow-10-in-2026-as-afcfta-implementation-accelerates", "https://news.un.org/en/node/1069081"], "mem": {"tag": "Africa's paper market", "story": "54 countries sign → trading starts in 2021 → but only about 15% of Africa's trade is within Africa → red tape is the real barrier", "numbers": [["54", "countries that have signed AfCFTA"], ["15%", "share of Africa's trade that is between African countries"], ["$450bn", "World Bank estimate of the income gain by 2035"]], "use": "free trade areas, non-tariff barriers, trade facilitation", "but": "Signing isn't trading: roads, border delays and paperwork matter more than tariffs."}}, {"id": "wto-appellate-body-crisis", "title": "The WTO court with no judges", "region": "Global (WTO, 166 members)", "years": "2019–2026", "subtopic": "4.4", "concepts": ["WTO", "dispute settlement", "multilateralism", "appeal into the void", "plurilateral agreements", "MPIA"], "hook": "Since December 2019 the WTO's highest court has had no judges, so any country that loses a case can appeal into a void and ignore the ruling.", "facts": ["The Appellate Body has not worked since 11 Dec 2019 because the US has blocked the appointment of new judges. The proposal to restart selections has been blocked nearly 100 times.", "More than 20 panel reports are reportedly stuck 'in the void', including the ruling on Indonesia's nickel ban (DS592).", "Workaround: the MPIA stand-in appeal system had 56 members by May 2025, including the EU, China, Japan, Canada and Brazil. The UK joined in Jun 2025.", "The 2026 Ministerial Conference in Yaoundé ended with no joint declaration, and 66 members moved ahead with their own e-commerce deal."], "diagram": "None", "evaluation": ["Without enforcement, WTO rules are weaker. This helps explain unilateral measures such as Indonesia's export ban.", "Critics say the court overreached and that WTO rules suit state-led economies such as China poorly. There is a real case for reform, not just restoration.", "Fragmentation: the MPIA and deals among smaller groups keep cooperation alive among willing members, but this is no longer universal multilateralism.", "The consensus rule cuts both ways: it protects small countries but lets a single member block everyone, as the 2026 conference showed."], "rating": {"relevance": 5, "data": 3, "recency": 5, "evaluation": 5}, "verdict": "Essential for any WTO evaluation question. Weakness: the latest counts (blocked proposals, MPIA members) are not fully verified, so use rounded figures.", "sources": ["https://www.hinrichfoundation.com/research/article/trade-governance/mc14-exposes-wto-challenges", "https://ielp.worldtradelaw.net/2025/06/the-uk-joins-the-mpia/", "https://ustr.gov/about/policy-offices/press-office/press-releases/2026/march/press-release-regarding-wtos-14th-ministerial-conference"], "mem": {"tag": "Court with no judges", "story": "New appeal judges blocked → WTO appeal court stops (Dec 2019) → losers 'appeal into the void' → rulings can't be enforced", "numbers": [["2019", "year the WTO appeal court stopped working"], ["56", "members of the stand-in appeal system (MPIA), May 2025"], ["166", "WTO member countries"]], "use": "the WTO, dispute settlement, multilateralism", "but": "Critics argue the court overreached, so the system may need reform rather than just restoring."}}, {"id": "eu-cbam", "title": "CBAM: a carbon tariff in disguise?", "region": "European Union (vs. India, China, Turkey, Russia exporters)", "years": "2023–2027", "subtopic": "4.2, 4.3", "concepts": ["administrative barrier", "border tax / tariff-equivalent", "carbon leakage", "negative externality", "level playing field", "WTO compatibility"], "hook": "From 2026 the EU charges importers of steel, aluminium, cement and fertiliser for their carbon emissions. Brussels calls it climate policy; India calls it discriminatory protectionism.", "facts": ["The reporting-only phase began 1 Oct 2023 and the definitive phase started 1 Jan 2026. It covers iron and steel, aluminium, cement, fertilisers, electricity and hydrogen.", "A 2025 simplification added a 50-tonne annual import threshold. Certificate sales open 1 Feb 2027, and the first payment for 2026 imports is due 30 Sep 2027.", "The certificate price follows the EU carbon market price, reported at about €75 per tonne of CO₂ in the first half of 2026. EU producers' free carbon allowances are cut by 2.5% a year in 2026–27.", "Proposed extension to about 180 downstream steel- and aluminium-intensive products from 2028.", "India has said it will challenge CBAM at the WTO. One study estimated Indian steel could face €407m–762m a year in CBAM costs by 2034."], "diagram": "Tariff diagram (CBAM acts like a tariff on high-carbon imports); negative production externality diagram for the justification", "evaluation": ["Justification: it corrects a negative externality and prevents carbon leakage, so it is not protection for its own sake. It is arguably WTO-compatible because it mirrors the EU's domestic carbon price.", "Developing-country view: it raises costs for poorer exporters who did not cause historical emissions, and acts as a non-tariff barrier. This is a fairness argument.", "Compliance costs: reporting embedded emissions is an administrative barrier in itself, falling hardest on small exporters, which is why the 50-tonne threshold was added.", "Uncertain effectiveness: exporters may send clean output to the EU and dirty output elsewhere, limiting the climate benefit."], "rating": {"relevance": 4, "data": 3, "recency": 5, "evaluation": 5}, "verdict": "An unusual administrative or green barrier with strong evaluation potential. Weakness: it is technical, no money has been paid yet (first bill 2027), and price figures come from secondary sources.", "sources": ["https://icapcarbonaction.com/en/news/eu-cbam-enters-compliance-phase-and-outlines-path-ahead", "https://www.zerocarbon.org.in/blog/cbam-2026-what-changed-first-bill-2027", "https://climatechangenews.com/2024/08/01/indian-official-calls-eu-carbon-border-tax-unfair-and-unacceptable/"], "mem": {"tag": "Carbon border tax", "story": "EU makes its own firms pay for carbon → from 2026 importers must pay too → stops 'carbon leakage' → India calls it protectionism", "numbers": [["2026", "year CBAM charges began applying to imports"], ["€75", "approximate carbon price per tonne of CO₂ in 2026"], ["50", "tonnes a year: smaller importers are exempt"]], "use": "administrative barriers, negative externalities, carbon leakage", "but": "It raises costs for poorer exporters who did not cause historical emissions."}}, {"id": "brexit-uk-eu-reset", "title": "Brexit: leaving a common market", "region": "United Kingdom & European Union", "years": "2016–2026", "subtopic": "4.4 (also 4.2)", "concepts": ["common market", "customs union", "free trade agreement", "non-tariff barriers", "administrative barriers", "dynamic alignment"], "hook": "The UK went from the EU single market to a zero-tariff free trade agreement, and found that paperwork, not tariffs, was the real barrier.", "facts": ["From 1 Jan 2021 the UK left the single market and customs union. The Trade and Cooperation Agreement has zero tariffs and zero quotas, but adds customs declarations, rules of origin and food safety checks.", "The OBR assumes UK exports and imports will be about 15% lower in the long run than if the UK had stayed in the EU, and productivity about 4% lower.", "May 2025 UK–EU 'reset': agreement in principle on a food and farm (SPS) deal based on following EU rules. The UK government estimates it adds up to 0.14% to GDP (about £5.1bn) by 2040.", "The next UK–EU summit is set for 20 Nov 2026 in Brussels, and the food and farm deal is targeted for mid-2027."], "diagram": "None standard (a non-tariff barrier can be shown as a cost increase that shifts exporters' supply left)", "evaluation": ["Integration ladder: shows clearly the difference between a free trade agreement and a common market. Removing tariffs alone does not remove trade costs.", "Sovereignty vs market access: the food and farm deal brings back EU rule-taking in exchange for lower trade costs. This is a genuine political trade-off, not just economics.", "Contested evidence: the OBR's 15% and 4% figures are model-based and critics dispute them. COVID and the energy shock make it hard to isolate Brexit's effect.", "Stakeholders: small exporters (food, fresh produce) were hit hardest by fixed paperwork costs, while large firms could absorb them."], "rating": {"relevance": 5, "data": 4, "recency": 4, "evaluation": 5}, "verdict": "A classic that examiners know well, made current by the 2025–26 reset. Weakness: much of the evidence is contested and model-based, and the reset's outcome is still pending.", "sources": ["https://obr.uk/box/how-are-our-brexit-forecasting-assumptions-performing/", "https://www.fruitnet.com/fresh-produce-journal/eu-uk-reset-summit-confirmed-for-november/272983.article", "https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/682f38c3baff3dab99775240/DBT_SPS_GDP_Impact_method_note.odt"], "mem": {"tag": "Brexit paperwork", "story": "UK leaves the single market (2021) → zero tariffs but customs forms and food checks → trade about 15% lower in the long run → 2025 'reset' to cut checks", "numbers": [["15%", "OBR estimate of how much lower UK trade will be in the long run"], ["4%", "OBR estimate of lost productivity"], ["2021", "year the UK left the single market and customs union"]], "use": "common market vs free trade agreement, non-tariff barriers", "but": "The 15% is a model estimate, and COVID makes Brexit's effect hard to isolate."}}, {"id": "japan-yen-intervention", "title": "Yen at 160: Japan's ¥27 trillion defence", "region": "Japan", "years": "2024–2026", "subtopic": "4.5, 4.6", "concepts": ["managed float", "central bank intervention", "interest rate differentials", "carry trade", "imported (cost-push) inflation", "current account: primary income", "HL: exchange rate calculations"], "hook": "Japan has spent more than ¥27 trillion in 2026 buying its own currency, and the yen is still close to 160 per dollar.", "facts": ["2024: Japan intervened twice. It spent ¥9.79tn (late April to late May) and ¥5.53tn (July) after the yen reached about ¥161.95/$, its weakest since 1986.", "2026: ¥11.73tn was spent between late April and late May, but the yen still fell to ¥163.24/$ in July. Japan then spent a record ¥15.4tn from 30 July to late August, and the yen strengthened 4.4% in early August.", "Total intervention in 2026 is over ¥27tn. The yen was back past 160 by late August and at about 159 in mid-October 2026.", "Interest gap: the Bank of Japan raised its rate to 1.25% in September 2026, its highest since 1995, but that is still well below rates in other major economies, so money keeps flowing out of yen.", "Current account: a record ¥34.52tn surplus in FY2025, but almost all of it was investment income (¥42.28tn). The goods balance was only +¥1.36tn."], "diagram": "Currency market for yen: a wider interest gap shifts demand for yen left and supply of yen right, so the yen depreciates. Intervention (selling dollar reserves to buy yen) shifts demand for yen right. Optional AD/AS: SRAS shifts left from dearer imported energy.", "evaluation": ["Intervention treats the symptom, not the cause. While the interest rate gap persists, the effect fades within weeks: the yen was past 160 again less than a month later. Reserves are finite, and raising rates faster makes Japan's huge public debt more expensive.", "Winners: exporters and inbound tourism. Losers: households and firms buying imported energy and food, made worse by the 2026 oil shock.", "Marshall–Lerner and J-curve: years of weak yen produced only a small goods surplus. Many Japanese firms produce abroad, so export volumes respond weakly, and the record surplus comes from investment income, whose yen value rises when the yen falls."], "rating": {"relevance": 5, "data": 5, "recency": 5, "evaluation": 5}, "verdict": "The best all-round case of a managed float and the limits of intervention, with clean numbers for HL calculations. Weakness: the story moves fast, so check the latest yen rate before the exam.", "sources": ["https://www.nbcnews.com/world/asia/us-dollar-falls-sharply-japanese-yen-market-intervention-rcna590522", "https://www.nippon.com/en/news/yjj2026052901037/", "https://english.adnkronos.com/2026/05/13/japans-current-account-surplus-hits-record-high-in-fy-2025/"], "mem": {"tag": "Yen at 160", "story": "Japan's interest rates far below others' → money leaves the yen → yen falls past 160 per dollar → Japan spends ¥27tn buying yen → it slips back", "numbers": [["160", "yen per dollar, the level Japan keeps defending"], ["¥27tn", "spent buying yen in 2026"], ["1.25%", "Bank of Japan interest rate, Sept 2026"]], "use": "managed float, intervention, interest rate differentials", "but": "Intervention fades within weeks while the interest rate gap stays."}}, {"id": "argentina-peso-band", "title": "Argentina: from a 54% devaluation to a crawling band", "region": "Argentina", "years": "2023–2026", "subtopic": "4.5, 4.6", "concepts": ["devaluation", "parallel (black) market", "crawling peg", "exchange rate band", "inflation pass-through", "foreign currency reserves", "IMF lending", "speculation"], "hook": "In one televised address in 2023 Argentina cut the peso's value by more than half. Three years on, the peso floats inside a band whose limits move with inflation.", "facts": ["12 December 2023: the official rate moved from 366.5 to 800 pesos/$. The peso lost 54% of its dollar value. A 2% monthly crawling peg followed, cut to 1% a month from February 2025.", "Inflation: 211.4% in 2023, 117.8% in 2024, 31.5% in 2025 (lowest since 2017). It has crept back up to 33.4% in September 2026.", "April 2025: most currency controls were lifted. The peso now floats in a band (initially 1,000–1,400 pesos/$), backed by a $20bn IMF loan. Since January 2026 the band limits move monthly with inflation.", "October 2026: the peso was about 1,519/$, inside a band of 722–1,925. The official and black-market rates are less than 1% apart."], "diagram": "Currency market with upper and lower band limits: the central bank sells dollars at the ceiling and buys them at the floor. The devaluation is shown as resetting an overvalued fixed rate to near equilibrium. AD/AS for cost-push inflation.", "evaluation": ["The devaluation closed the gap with the black market and restored competitiveness. The cost was an inflation spike and a sharp fall in real wages in 2024.", "A crawling peg slower than inflation means real appreciation, which erodes competitiveness. That is why the 2025 switch to a band, and the 2026 inflation-linked limits, matter.", "Credibility has relied on IMF loans and other outside money. Is the regime sustainable without repeated rescues?", "Buying dollars builds reserves but injects pesos (an inflation risk), and inflation re-accelerated slightly in 2026. A short-run versus long-run trade-off."], "rating": {"relevance": 5, "data": 5, "recency": 5, "evaluation": 5}, "verdict": "Covers fixed, crawling and band regimes, devaluation, reserves and IMF finance in one country, with excellent numbers. Weakness: it is complex, so pick the one regime the question asks about.", "sources": ["https://www.bnnbloomberg.ca/argentina-to-target-2-monthly-devaluation-of-peso-1.2010872", "https://www.bcra.gob.ar/en/news/deepening-of-the-monetary-aggregate-scheme-re-monetization-phase-2026/", "https://www.riotimesonline.com/argentina-reserves-peso-band-imf-2026/"], "mem": {"tag": "Peso halved overnight", "story": "Peso badly overvalued → cut 54% in one day (Dec 2023) → inflation spikes, then falls → peso now floats inside a band", "numbers": [["54%", "value the peso lost in one day, Dec 2023"], ["211%", "Argentina's inflation in 2023"], ["31.5%", "inflation in 2025 after stabilisation"]], "use": "devaluation, crawling peg, exchange rate band", "but": "Real wages fell sharply, and stability has relied on IMF loans."}}, {"id": "egypt-float-ras-el-hekma", "title": "Egypt's float and the Ras El-Hekma lifeline", "region": "Egypt", "years": "2024–2026", "subtopic": "4.5, 4.6", "concepts": ["move from peg to float", "devaluation and parallel market", "interest rate rise to defend currency", "FDI and IMF inflows (financial account)", "remittances", "financing a current account deficit"], "hook": "Egypt let its pound fall 38% in a single day in March 2024. A $35bn UAE property deal made it possible.", "facts": ["6 March 2024: the pound was floated, moving from about 30.9 to just over 50 per $ that day, a loss of about 38% of its value. The central bank raised interest rates by 6 percentage points.", "The same day the IMF programme was raised from $3bn to $8bn. In February 2024 the UAE signed the Ras El-Hekma deal, about $35bn of FDI.", "FY2025/26: the current account deficit widened to $22.5bn from $15.4bn, yet the overall balance of payments showed a $2.8bn surplus. Net FDI rose to $18bn and portfolio inflows to $4.2bn.", "Credit items: remittances +29.6% to $47.3bn, Suez Canal receipts +21.6% to $4.4bn, tourism +10.3% to $18.4bn.", "The pound was about EGP50.7/$ in July 2026, with central bank rates of 19–20%."], "diagram": "Currency market: an official rate held above equilibrium creates a dollar shortage and a parallel market; floating moves the rate to equilibrium. Also a BoP table showing a current account deficit financed by financial account surpluses (FDI, portfolio, IMF).", "evaluation": ["The float ended dollar shortages and pulled remittances into formal banks (+29.6%). This is a clear benefit of a market-clearing rate over a peg the country cannot defend.", "The current account deficit still widened to $22.5bn. It is financed by FDI, reversible 'hot money' and IMF loans, so its sustainability depends on investor confidence.", "Selling strategic assets to foreign investors brings dollars now but means investment-income outflows later.", "Egypt imports much of its food and fuel, so devaluation fed cost-push inflation and hit poorer households. Suez revenue is exposed to regional conflict beyond policy control."], "rating": {"relevance": 5, "data": 5, "recency": 5, "evaluation": 5}, "verdict": "Excellent for linking 4.5 and 4.6: a float plus a textbook current account deficit financed by the financial account, with recent full-year data. Weakness: the latest pound rate found is from July 2026.", "sources": ["https://www.ksat.com/business/2024/03/06/egypt-floats-its-currency-and-agrees-with-the-imf-to-increase-a-bailout-loan-to-8-billion/", "https://www.thestar.com.my/news/world/2026/10/13/egypt-posts-28-bln-usd-balance-of-payments-surplus-in-fy-20252026", "https://capital.com/en-gb/analysis/usd-to-egp-forecast-dollar-egyptian-pound"], "mem": {"tag": "Egypt's 38% float", "story": "Dollar shortage → $35bn UAE deal plus $8bn IMF loan → pound floated, −38% in a day → remittances flow back into banks", "numbers": [["38%", "value the Egyptian pound lost on 6 March 2024"], ["$35bn", "UAE investment in the Ras El-Hekma deal"], ["$22.5bn", "Egypt's current account deficit, FY2025/26"]], "use": "floating a currency, devaluation, financing a current account deficit", "but": "The deficit is financed by FDI, hot money and IMF loans, which can dry up."}}, {"id": "nigeria-naira-float", "title": "Nigeria's naira: float, slump and slow recovery", "region": "Nigeria", "years": "2023–2026", "subtopic": "4.5, 4.6", "concepts": ["exchange rate unification / float", "depreciation", "imported inflation", "Marshall–Lerner condition", "valuation effects on trade data", "foreign exchange reserves"], "hook": "The naira lost about two-thirds of its value after the 2023 float. Nigeria's trade surplus soared in naira, mostly because oil is priced in dollars.", "facts": ["June 2023: the government unified the exchange rate. The official rate went from ₦463/$ (June 2023) to about ₦1,484/$ (June 2024), a loss of about 69% of the naira's dollar value.", "Q1 2024 trade surplus: ₦6.52tn (+79.1% on the previous quarter). Exports were ₦19.2tn against ₦6.48tn a year earlier, a jump driven largely by the exchange rate.", "Inflation reached 34.19% in June 2024, the highest since 1996. The central bank raised its policy rate to 27.5%.", "2026: the naira is firmer at about ₦1,346/$ (August 2026). Reserves were $51.92bn in July 2026, the highest in more than 17 years."], "diagram": "Currency market: removing an overvalued official rate moves it to equilibrium (a large depreciation). J-curve and Marshall–Lerner discussion. AD/AS: SRAS shifts left from dearer imports.", "evaluation": ["Marshall–Lerner check: oil dominates exports and is priced in dollars, and output is limited by production problems, so export volumes barely respond to a cheaper naira. Much of the surplus boom is a valuation effect (the same dollars are worth more naira).", "Losers: households facing inflation above 30% and import-dependent firms. Winners: exporters and government revenue measured in naira.", "By 2026 the float looks more successful, with a stable naira and record reserves. Higher oil prices and FX reforms both helped, so it is hard to say which mattered more.", "A lasting current account improvement needs supply-side change (domestic refining, non-oil exports), not just a cheaper currency."], "rating": {"relevance": 5, "data": 4, "recency": 4, "evaluation": 5}, "verdict": "A strong case for questioning whether depreciation improves the trade balance (Marshall–Lerner), with a 2026 recovery twist. Weakness: Nigeria rebased its inflation index in 2025, so old and new figures are not directly comparable.", "sources": ["https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/trade-surplus-hits-14-yr-high-as-weak-naira-lifts-exports/", "https://techeconomy.ng/dollar-to-naira-exchange-rate-today-august-26-2026", "https://businessday.ng/news/article/cbn-retains-interest-rate-at-27-5-despite-slowing-inflation/"], "mem": {"tag": "Naira slump", "story": "Naira floated (2023) → loses about 69% → inflation hits 34% → trade surplus 'soars' only because oil is priced in dollars", "numbers": [["69%", "value the naira lost, June 2023 to June 2024"], ["34.19%", "Nigeria's inflation peak, June 2024"], ["27.5%", "Nigeria's central bank interest rate"]], "use": "depreciation, Marshall–Lerner condition, imported inflation", "but": "Oil volumes barely rose, so the trade 'boom' was mostly a currency effect."}}, {"id": "turkey-lira-inflation", "title": "Turkish lira: inflation differentials in action", "region": "Turkey (Türkiye)", "years": "2023–2026", "subtopic": "4.5, 4.6", "concepts": ["depreciation caused by relative inflation", "interest rate defence", "capital flight after political shocks", "reserve sales (intervention)", "real appreciation", "structural current account deficit"], "hook": "The lira has fallen every year, but since 2023 it has fallen more slowly than prices have risen, a deliberate 'real appreciation' to squeeze out inflation.", "facts": ["After the 2023 election Turkey returned to orthodox policy. The policy rate rose from 8.5% (June 2023) to 50% (March 2024).", "March 2025: after the detention of Istanbul's mayor, the lira hit a record of about 42/$. The central bank sold reserves (estimates reach about $50bn) and raised its rate to 46%.", "The lira went from about 42.9/$ (end of 2025) to about 49.6/$ (October 2026), a 13.6% depreciation. That is well below official inflation of 29.73%, so the lira appreciated in real terms.", "22 October 2026: rate cut from 37% to 36%, the first in nine months. Rates had been held because of the 2026 energy price shock.", "The 12-month current account deficit was $42.1bn (August 2026). Excluding energy and gold, the balance is in surplus."], "diagram": "Currency market: higher relative inflation reduces foreign demand for Turkish goods (demand for lira shifts left) and raises Turkish demand for imports (supply of lira shifts right), so the lira depreciates. A rate hike attracts hot money and shifts demand for lira right.", "evaluation": ["High rates slowed depreciation and drew in portfolio flows but cost growth. Credibility is fragile: one political shock in March 2025 triggered capital flight and huge reserve sales.", "Real appreciation cuts imported inflation but makes exporters (textiles, cars) less competitive. A direct trade-off between inflation and the current account.", "The deficit is structural: demand for energy and gold imports is price-inelastic, so expenditure switching through a weaker lira does little. Supply-side energy policy (renewables, nuclear) is the long-run fix.", "Data reliability: an independent group estimated September 2026 inflation at 46.6% against the official 29.7%. A useful point when evaluating official statistics."], "rating": {"relevance": 5, "data": 5, "recency": 5, "evaluation": 5}, "verdict": "Excellent for the link from inflation to the exchange rate, and for the limits of expenditure switching when imports are energy. Weakness: the March 2025 reserve-sale total is an estimate.", "sources": ["https://www.turkishminute.com/2026/10/22/turkey-cuts-interest-rate-to-36-percent-in-first-reduction-in-9-months/amp/", "https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2025/04/turkey-hikes-interest-rate-46-amid-turmoil-over-imamoglus-arrest", "https://tradingeconomics.com/turkey/current-account/news/574973"], "mem": {"tag": "Lira vs inflation", "story": "High inflation → lira keeps falling → interest rate raised to 50% → lira now falls slower than prices rise = real appreciation", "numbers": [["50%", "Turkey's interest rate peak, March 2024"], ["13.6%", "lira depreciation in 2026 to October"], ["29.73%", "official inflation, Sept 2026"]], "use": "inflation differentials, interest rate defence, structural current account deficit", "but": "Energy and gold imports barely respond to a weaker lira, so the deficit is structural."}}, {"id": "hong-kong-peg-both-sides", "title": "Hong Kong: defending both ends of the peg", "region": "Hong Kong SAR (China)", "years": "2025–2026", "subtopic": "4.5", "concepts": ["fixed exchange rate (currency board)", "intervention and money supply", "loss of independent monetary policy", "interest rate differentials and carry trade", "credibility and reserves"], "hook": "In 2025 the HKMA had to stop the Hong Kong dollar rising in May and stop it falling in June: the same peg defended from both ends within eight weeks.", "facts": ["The HK$ has been linked to the US$ since 1983 and trades in a band of 7.75–7.85 per US$. The HKMA must buy or sell HK$ at the limits.", "Early May 2025: strong inflows pushed the HK$ to the strong-side limit four times. The HKMA sold HK$129.4bn, and bank liquidity jumped from HK$44.7bn to HK$174.1bn.", "The extra liquidity pushed HK interest rates far below US rates, so carry trades followed. The weak-side limit was hit 12 times between late June and mid-August 2025, and the HKMA bought back HK$119.9bn.", "By early October 2026 the HK$ was about 7.846, close to the weak side again because of a widening HK$–US$ interest rate gap."], "diagram": "Currency market with a fixed band: at 7.85 the HKMA buys HK$ (demand shifts right) and at 7.75 it sells HK$ (supply shifts right). Link to the money market: intervention changes HK$ liquidity and interest rates.", "evaluation": ["The peg provides certainty for a trade and finance hub, and huge reserves make it credible, unlike Egypt or Argentina before their floats.", "The cost is imported US monetary policy: HK rates follow the Fed even when Hong Kong's property market or growth needs something different.", "The adjustment is automatic: intervention changes liquidity, interest rates move, and the flows reverse. But volatile interest rates hit mortgage borrowers.", "Without exchange rate flexibility, adjustment to shocks happens through prices, wages and asset values. A useful comparison with Bulgaria and with floating regimes."], "rating": {"relevance": 5, "data": 5, "recency": 5, "evaluation": 4}, "verdict": "Clean, official numbers showing how a fixed rate is maintained. Weakness: less drama for evaluation than crisis cases.", "sources": ["https://www.hkma.gov.hk/media/eng/publication-and-research/annual-report/2025/14_Monetary_Stability.pdf", "https://www.hkma.gov.hk/media/eng/publication-and-research/quarterly-bulletin/qb202509/Chapter4.pdf", "https://www.thestandard.com.hk/finance/article/344769/Widening-HKD-USD-rate-gap-pushes-HKD-towards-78500-weak-side-CU-HKMA"], "mem": {"tag": "Peg, both ends", "story": "HK$ fixed at 7.75–7.85 per US$ → May 2025: too strong, HKMA sells HK$ → June 2025: too weak, HKMA buys them back", "numbers": [["7.85", "weak-side limit of the HK$ band"], ["7.75", "strong-side limit of the HK$ band"], ["1983", "year the HK$ was pegged"]], "use": "fixed exchange rates, intervention, loss of independent monetary policy", "but": "Hong Kong must copy another country's interest rates even when its own economy needs something different."}}, {"id": "swiss-franc-safe-haven", "title": "Swiss franc: the safe haven that's too safe", "region": "Switzerland", "years": "2015; 2025–2026", "subtopic": "4.5", "concepts": ["safe-haven capital inflows", "appreciation", "abandoning a currency floor", "central bank intervention", "zero interest rates", "non-price competitiveness", "deflation risk"], "hook": "On 15 January 2015 the Swiss National Bank scrapped its euro floor and the franc jumped as much as 30% in minutes. A decade later it is again fighting a currency that is too strong.", "facts": ["Classic case: from 2011 the SNB held a floor of CHF1.20 per euro. On 15 January 2015 it abandoned the floor and cut rates to −0.75%. The franc rose up to about 30% within minutes, and the SNB booked a record CHF50bn loss in the first half of 2015.", "June 2025: with the franc strong and inflation near zero, the SNB cut its interest rate to 0%.", "2026: the franc hit about 0.90 per euro in March, its strongest since 2015, as investors looked for safety during the Gulf war. It traded around 0.93 in October.", "Swiss inflation is near zero (0.1% in early 2026), and the SNB held its rate at 0% in September 2026."], "diagram": "Currency market for CHF: safe-haven demand shifts demand for francs right, so the franc appreciates. SNB sales of francs shift supply right. The 2015 case can be drawn as a removed price floor.", "evaluation": ["A strong franc lowers import prices (near-zero inflation, deflation risk) but squeezes exporters and tourism. Swiss exports (watches, pharma, machinery) compete on quality, so demand is price-inelastic and the damage is smaller than theory suggests.", "The SNB's tools are limited: rates are already 0%, negative rates hurt banks and savers, and heavy intervention invites accusations of currency manipulation.", "Lesson from 2015: holding a rate against market forces builds up huge reserves and risk, and a sudden exit damages credibility."], "rating": {"relevance": 5, "data": 4, "recency": 4, "evaluation": 4}, "verdict": "A rare 'currency too strong' case pairing a classic (2015) with a 2025–26 update. Good for showing that appreciation has costs. Weakness: 2026 franc levels come from market commentary.", "sources": ["https://gulfnews.com/business/swiss-central-bank-books-record-loss-of-50b-francs-1.1559167", "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2025-11-14/us-switzerland-essentially-reached-trade-deal-greer-says", "https://www.fxstreet.com/news/chf-safe-haven-puzzle-and-snb-signals-commerzbank-202604140606"], "mem": {"tag": "Too-safe franc", "story": "Crisis → investors buy francs → franc too strong → SNB holds a floor of 1.20 per euro → drops it in 2015, franc jumps 30% in minutes", "numbers": [["1.20", "francs per euro: the floor the SNB defended until 2015"], ["30%", "the franc's jump in minutes on 15 Jan 2015"], ["0%", "SNB interest rate in 2025–26"]], "use": "safe-haven flows, appreciation, abandoning a currency floor", "but": "Swiss exports compete on quality, so a strong franc hurts less than theory predicts."}}, {"id": "china-trillion-surplus", "title": "China's trillion-dollar trade surplus", "region": "China (with Germany comparison)", "years": "2024–2026", "subtopic": "4.6, 4.5", "concepts": ["persistent current account surplus", "capital outflows (financial account deficit)", "managed exchange rate", "trade diversion", "global imbalances and protectionism"], "hook": "In 2025 China became the first country ever to run a goods trade surplus above $1 trillion, and by September 2026 it was on course to do it again.", "facts": ["2025 goods trade surplus: $1.189tn (2024: $993bn). Exports rose 5.5% to about $3.8tn while imports were flat at about $2.6tn, with exports redirected towards Southeast Asia, Africa and Europe.", "2025 current account surplus: $734.9bn (about 3.8% of GDP), matched by a financial account deficit of about $760bn.", "January–September 2026 surplus: about $938bn. September 2026 exports rose 36.3% to $447.2bn.", "The yuan was about 6.72/$ in late August 2026, its strongest since February 2023, even though the central bank set weaker daily rates to slow the rise.", "Comparison: Germany's 2025 current account surplus was €203bn (about 4.5% of GDP), €52bn lower than in 2024."], "diagram": "BoP identity (current account surplus = financial account outflows). Managed exchange rate: the central bank limits appreciation by selling yuan (supply of yuan shifts right).", "evaluation": ["Causes go beyond the exchange rate: high household saving, weak domestic consumption, industrial subsidies and overcapacity. Yuan appreciation alone would shrink the surplus only slowly.", "Consequences for China: export-led growth and jobs, but dependence on foreign demand and rising protectionism abroad.", "Consequences for others: cheap imports benefit consumers but threaten manufacturing jobs, and China 'exporting deflation' strains partners' industries.", "Surpluses are not permanent: Germany's is shrinking as its car industry loses competitiveness. A surplus reflects competitiveness and saving choices, not automatically success."], "rating": {"relevance": 5, "data": 5, "recency": 5, "evaluation": 5}, "verdict": "The best current example of a persistent surplus, with headline numbers examiners will recognise. Weakness: don't confuse the $1.19tn goods trade surplus with the $735bn current account surplus.", "sources": ["https://finance.yahoo.com/news/chinas-trade-ends-2025-record-031557801.html", "https://www.bofit.fi/en/monitoring/weekly/2026/vw202616_2/", "https://www.fxstreet.com/analysis/chinas-export-engine-continued-to-hum-in-september-202610140756"], "mem": {"tag": "Trillion-dollar surplus", "story": "High saving + weak spending + subsidised factories → exports far exceed imports → first goods surplus over $1 trillion (2025)", "numbers": [["$1.189tn", "China's goods trade surplus in 2025"], ["$734.9bn", "China's current account surplus in 2025"], ["€203bn", "Germany's 2025 current account surplus, for comparison"]], "use": "persistent current account surplus, global imbalances", "but": "Don't mix up the $1.19tn trade surplus with the $735bn current account surplus."}}, {"id": "bulgaria-joins-euro", "title": "Bulgaria swaps the lev for the euro", "region": "Bulgaria (EU)", "years": "1997–2026", "subtopic": "4.5, 4.4", "concepts": ["fixed exchange rate / currency board", "credibility and inflation control", "monetary union", "loss of independent monetary policy", "transaction costs and exchange rate risk", "changeover inflation"], "hook": "Bulgaria fixed its currency for 28 years to escape hyperinflation, and on 1 January 2026 the lev disappeared altogether.", "facts": ["A currency board was set up in July 1997, with every lev backed by foreign reserves, after monthly inflation reached about 242% in February 1997. The peg moved to the euro in 1999 at 1.95583 leva per euro.", "The euro replaced the lev on 1 January 2026 at the same 1.95583 rate (a 100-lev price became €51.13). Shops had to show prices in both currencies for a year.", "The European Commission's 2025 Convergence Report found Bulgaria met all the criteria for joining.", "Inflation after the switch: 5.7% in September 2026, among the highest in the eurozone, with energy prices a major factor."], "diagram": "Fixed exchange rate shown on a currency market (rate held at 1.95583 by the currency board), then fixed permanently in monetary union. AD/AS for inflation effects.", "evaluation": ["Benefits: no exchange rate risk or conversion costs with the EU (Bulgaria's main market), lower borrowing costs, more FDI and a seat on the ECB's council.", "Costs are smaller than for most joiners because Bulgaria gave up independent monetary policy in 1997. But the ECB's single rate may be too loose for a faster-growing, higher-inflation economy.", "Changeover inflation: the government says price rises are not caused by the euro, while the public sees rounding-up. The 2026 energy shock makes the causes hard to separate.", "Bulgaria can never devalue to fix a future current account deficit, so it must rely on cutting wages and prices relative to others, plus supply-side policy."], "rating": {"relevance": 4, "data": 4, "recency": 5, "evaluation": 4}, "verdict": "A fresh 2026 example of fixed rates and monetary union with a strong backstory. Weakness: it overlaps with economic integration (4.4) more than exchange rates.", "sources": ["https://trade.ec.europa.eu/access-to-markets/en/news/bulgaria-adopts-euro-1-january-2026", "https://www.bta.bg/en/news/921311-july-1-1997-currency-board-goes-into-effect-pegging-lev-to-deutsche-mark", "https://tradingeconomics.com/bulgaria/inflation-cpi/news/591846"], "mem": {"tag": "Lev to euro", "story": "Hyperinflation (1997) → currency board pegs the lev → 28 years fixed → euro replaces the lev on 1 January 2026", "numbers": [["1.95583", "leva per euro, the fixed rate"], ["242%", "Bulgaria's monthly inflation, Feb 1997"], ["2026", "year Bulgaria adopted the euro"]], "use": "fixed exchange rates, currency boards, monetary union", "but": "Bulgaria can never devalue again, so shocks must be absorbed through wages and prices."}}, {"id": "hormuz-oil-shock-tot", "title": "The Hormuz shock: oil importers vs oil exporters", "region": "Global: India (importer) vs Norway (exporter)", "years": "2026", "subtopic": "4.6, 4.5 (HL terms of trade)", "concepts": ["terms of trade", "commodity price shock", "price-inelastic demand for imports", "currency depreciation of importers", "managed float and intervention", "cost-push inflation", "current account effects"], "hook": "When the Strait of Hormuz was disrupted in 2026, India's rupee hit record lows while Norway's trade surplus jumped by NOK 101bn in a single quarter.", "facts": ["A war involving Iran began on 28 February 2026. About a fifth of world oil normally passes through the Strait of Hormuz, and the IEA called it the largest supply disruption in the history of the oil market.", "Brent crude was about $64–67 in January 2026, peaked around $115–118 in late March, fell to about $70 by July after a ceasefire, and was back above $100 in October 2026.", "India imports about 90% of its crude. The rupee went from about 90/$ at the start of 2026 to a record low of about 97 in late May, down about 7%. The RBI raised its rate to 5.50% in October, its first hike since 2023.", "Norway: the Q2 2026 trade surplus was NOK 232bn, up NOK 101bn on a year earlier, because of higher oil and gas prices."], "diagram": "Terms of trade index = (index of export prices ÷ index of import prices) × 100. Currency market for the rupee: a bigger oil bill shifts supply of rupees right, so the rupee depreciates. AD/AS: SRAS shifts left for importers; AD shifts right for exporters.", "evaluation": ["Oil demand is price-inelastic in the short run, so a higher price raises the import bill. India's terms of trade worsen, the current account comes under pressure and depreciation adds more inflation, a vicious circle.", "Exporters gain only if they can ship: Gulf producers saw better prices but blocked volumes, while Norway gained. A terms of trade improvement is not the same as higher export revenue.", "Policy dilemma: RBI rate hikes support the rupee but slow growth. Holding down fuel prices protects consumers but costs the budget.", "Short run versus long run: the price fell to about $70 in July before rebounding. Persistent shocks encourage substitution (renewables, efficiency). HL practice: if import prices rise 20% and export prices 2%, ToT = 102/120 × 100 = 85, a 15% deterioration."], "rating": {"relevance": 5, "data": 4, "recency": 5, "evaluation": 5}, "verdict": "The defining terms of trade shock of your exam cycle, with importer and exporter sides in one example. Weakness: very fast-moving, so recheck oil prices and the rupee before exams.", "sources": ["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_Iran_war_fuel_crisis", "https://www.business-standard.com/finance/news/rbi-intervenes-to-support-rupee-as-it-nears-record-low-on-oil-price-surge-126072000317_1.html", "https://www.ssb.no/en/utenriksokonomi/utenriksregnskap/statistikk/utenriksregnskap/articles/very-high-net-iip-in-the-second-quarter-of-2026"], "mem": {"tag": "Hormuz oil shock", "story": "Gulf war disrupts Hormuz → oil jumps from $65 to $115+ → India's import bill rises, rupee hits record low → Norway's surplus jumps", "numbers": [["$115", "approximate oil (Brent) peak, March 2026, up from about $65"], ["90%", "share of India's crude oil that is imported"], ["NOK 101bn", "rise in Norway's quarterly trade surplus, Q2 2026"]], "use": "terms of trade, commodity price shocks, depreciation", "but": "Exporters only gain if they can ship: Gulf producers had high prices but blocked routes."}}, {"id": "ghana-cedi-gold-cocoa", "title": "Ghana's cedi: gold glitters, then oil bites", "region": "Ghana", "years": "2024–2026", "subtopic": "4.5 (HL terms of trade)", "concepts": ["terms of trade improvement", "appreciation", "imported disinflation", "IMF-backed stabilisation", "commodity dependence and volatility", "HL: percentage change calculations"], "hook": "With gold at about $3,400 an ounce, Ghana's cedi went from one of the world's worst currencies to its best performer in the first half of 2025.", "facts": ["Gold rose from about $2,000/oz (2024) to about $3,400 (May 2025), with cocoa prices also high. Gold export earnings were $11.6bn in 2024.", "By early June 2025 the cedi had gained nearly 50% against the dollar (about ₵15 to ₵10.21), the world's best performer. It ended 2025 at about ₵10.5–10.9/$ against ₵14.70 a year earlier.", "Inflation fell to 3.4% (April 2026), and the Bank of Ghana cut its policy rate to 14.0%.", "2026 reversal: the cedi was down 8.4% by mid-May (₵11.41/$) as the war raised the fuel import bill, even though the trade surplus was $5.28bn to April 2026."], "diagram": "Terms of trade: higher export prices increase demand for cedis (demand shifts right), so the cedi appreciates. AD/AS: cheaper imports shift SRAS right.", "evaluation": ["Was it the terms of trade or policy? The IMF programme and tight policy mattered too, so a good answer weighs both.", "Winners: consumers (inflation fell from over 20% to 3.4%) and the government (cheaper foreign-currency debt). Losers: other exporters and cocoa farmers paid in cedis.", "Commodity dependence means volatility: in 2026 the oil import bill reversed part of the gain. A risk of the resource curse.", "HL calculation trap: '₵14.70 to ₵10.50' means the dollar fell 28.6% in cedi terms, but the cedi's dollar value rose 40% (14.70 ÷ 10.50 = 1.40)."], "rating": {"relevance": 5, "data": 4, "recency": 5, "evaluation": 5}, "verdict": "A memorable positive terms of trade story with a 2026 reversal, ideal for balanced evaluation and HL percentage practice. Weakness: sources disagree on the size of the 2025 gain depending on the dates used.", "sources": ["https://www.dabafinance.com/en/news/ghanas-cedi-becomes-worlds-best-performing-currency-in-2025", "https://www.graphic.com.gh/business/business-news/cedi-depreciates-8-4-against-dollar-as-inflation-falls-to-3-4-bank-of-ghana-data.html", "https://african-markets.com/en/news/west-africa/ghana/ghanas-currency-best-performing-globally-boosted-by-imf-aid-oil-and-gold"], "mem": {"tag": "Golden cedi", "story": "Gold rises from $2,000 to $3,400 → more demand for cedis → cedi becomes the best performer of early 2025 → inflation falls to 3.4%", "numbers": [["$3,400", "gold price per ounce, May 2025"], ["50%", "approximate rise in the cedi against the dollar by June 2025"], ["3.4%", "Ghana's inflation, April 2026"]], "use": "terms of trade, appreciation", "but": "Was it gold prices or tight IMF-backed policy? Both mattered."}}, {"id": "uk-brexit-pound-jcurve", "title": "Brexit pound: the textbook J-curve (that wasn't quite)", "region": "United Kingdom", "years": "2016–2017", "subtopic": "4.6, 4.5", "concepts": ["depreciation driven by expectations", "imported inflation", "J-curve", "Marshall–Lerner condition", "primary income valuation effects", "HL: exchange rate calculations"], "hook": "On the night of the Brexit vote sterling fell from $1.50 to $1.32, one of the biggest one-day falls for a major currency since the Second World War.", "facts": ["23–24 June 2016: the pound fell from about $1.50 to as low as $1.32 overnight (lowest since 1985) and closed around $1.36. A flash crash in October 2016 took it to $1.15.", "HL calculation: $1.50 to $1.32 is a 12% depreciation. A $30,000 imported car rises from £20,000 to £22,727 (+13.6%).", "The current account deficit narrowed from a record 5.2% of GDP (2016) to 3.9% (2017). The main driver was the primary income deficit shrinking from 2.5% to 1.6% of GDP.", "Inflation reached 3.1% in November 2017, the highest since 2012, driven by the weak pound and oil. It was back to 2.5% by March 2018."], "diagram": "J-curve (trade balance against time after depreciation). Currency market: worse expectations shift demand for pounds left and supply right.", "evaluation": ["J-curve logic: contracts are fixed and demand is inelastic in the short run, so the trade balance worsens before volumes adjust. The twist: the UK current account improved mainly through primary income (foreign earnings worth more in pounds), not export volumes.", "Marshall–Lerner: the UK imports many necessities and exports high-value services, so short-run elasticities may sum to less than 1, limiting the improvement.", "Distribution: real wages were squeezed by 3.1% inflation, while exporters and multinationals with dollar earnings gained.", "A depreciation caused by a negative shock (uncertainty, lower investment) is not the same as a planned devaluation. Competitiveness gains were offset by weaker investment."], "rating": {"relevance": 5, "data": 5, "recency": 2, "evaluation": 5}, "verdict": "A classic examiners still reward for the J-curve and Marshall–Lerner, with a nuance (primary income, not trade, drove the improvement) that shows real evaluation. Weakness: it is ten years old, so pair it with a 2025–26 case.", "sources": ["https://money.cnn.com/2016/06/24/investing/pound-crash-eu-referendum/", "https://www.ons.gov.uk/economy/nationalaccounts/balanceofpayments/bulletins/unitedkingdombalanceofpaymentsthepinkbook/2018", "https://www.morningstar.co.uk/uk/news/163671/uk-inflation-hits-31.aspx"], "mem": {"tag": "Brexit pound J-curve", "story": "Leave vote → pound falls from $1.50 to $1.32 overnight → import prices up, inflation 3.1% → deficit narrows, mostly via investment income", "numbers": [["$1.32", "the pound's low on referendum night (from $1.50)"], ["12%", "size of the pound's overnight fall"], ["3.1%", "UK inflation peak, Nov 2017"]], "use": "J-curve, Marshall–Lerner condition, imported inflation", "but": "The current account improved mainly through investment income, not more exports."}}, {"id": "sdg-report-2026-off-track", "title": "A third on track: the SDGs at the 2026 checkpoint", "region": "Global", "years": "2015–2026", "subtopic": "4.7", "concepts": ["Sustainable Development Goals", "progress towards SDGs", "financing gap", "aid decline", "conflict and climate as barriers", "measuring SDG progress"], "hook": "With under five years to 2030, the UN says only 36% of measurable SDG targets are on track or making moderate progress, and 15% have gone backwards since 2015.", "facts": ["UN SDG Report 2026: of 139 targets with trend data, 36% are on track or moderately progressing, 49% are moving too slowly and 15% have slipped below their 2015 baselines.", "About 1 in 10 people still live in extreme poverty, 2.3 billion face food insecurity (more than in 2015), and none of the gender-equality targets are on track.", "Gains since 2015: nearly 1 billion more people have safely managed drinking water, internet use rose from 40% to 74%, and 134 countries have met the child-mortality target.", "Headwinds: aid fell by a record 23% in 2025, 118 million people are forcibly displaced, 2025 was 1.43°C above pre-industrial temperatures, and the annual SDG financing gap is about $4 trillion.", "The Sustainable Development Report 2026 (SDSN) is gloomier: only 16% of targets are projected to be met by 2030. Finland tops its SDG Index."], "diagram": "None standard. A PPC showing the trade-off between growth and environmental sustainability, or a negative production externality diagram for the climate goals, can help", "evaluation": ["Measurement matters: the UN (36%) and SDSN (16%) figures differ because they use different methods. This shows how sensitive aggregate indicators are to how they are built.", "Global averages hide very different regional results. East and South Asia have outperformed every other region since 2015, while conflict-affected sub-Saharan countries are going backwards.", "The UN blames aid cuts, conflict and climate, but domestic governance and debt burdens are also barriers (4.9), so failure can't be put down only to global financing.", "The SDGs have no enforcement mechanism. They coordinate effort but don't bind anyone, so missing the targets says as much about political will as about economics."], "rating": {"relevance": 5, "data": 5, "recency": 5, "evaluation": 4}, "verdict": "The best up-to-date opener for any 4.7 essay, with clean official numbers. Weakness: it is global and aggregate, so pair it with a country case to show a mechanism.", "sources": ["https://unstats.un.org/sdgs/files/report/2026/SDGs_Report_Key_Messages_2026.pdf", "https://www.unsdsn.org/resources/sustainable-development-report-2026"], "mem": {"tag": "SDGs off track", "story": "17 goals set in 2015 → 2026 check-up: only 36% of targets on track → 15% gone backwards → aid falling", "numbers": [["36%", "SDG targets on track or progressing (UN, 2026)"], ["15%", "SDG targets now worse than in 2015"], ["$4tn", "yearly SDG financing gap"]], "use": "Sustainable Development Goals, measuring progress", "but": "The UN says 36% but another report says 16%: the measure changes the verdict."}}, {"id": "guyana-vs-sri-lanka-hdi-gap", "title": "Same HDI, very different incomes: Guyana vs Sri Lanka", "region": "Guyana and Sri Lanka", "years": "2019–2026", "subtopic": "4.8 (also 4.9)", "concepts": ["GNI per capita vs HDI", "GNI per capita rank minus HDI rank", "composite vs single indicators", "PPP", "resource curse", "income classification"], "hook": "Guyana and Sri Lanka have exactly the same HDI (0.776), but Guyana's income per head is almost four times higher, the worst income-to-HDI gap in the world.", "facts": ["Human Development Report 2025 (2023 data): both rank 89th with an HDI of 0.776. Guyana's GNI per capita is $46,959 (PPP) against $12,616 for Sri Lanka.", "Guyana's GNI per capita rank minus HDI rank is −54, the largest negative gap of all 193 countries. Sri Lanka's is +22, and Cuba's is +30 on a GNI per capita of only $8,415.", "Oil boom: the IMF puts Guyana's growth at 43.6% in 2024 and 19.3% in 2025, with oil output above 900,000 barrels a day by end-2025.", "Sri Lanka defaulted on its debt in 2022, yet the World Bank reclassified it as upper-middle income on 1 July 2026.", "Guyana's Natural Resource Fund held over $3.1bn by end-2024."], "diagram": "None standard. AD/AS with an LRAS shift from the oil discovery, or a PPC shifting outward", "evaluation": ["Income turns into health and education with a lag. Guyana's oil income began only in 2019, while life expectancy and schooling build over decades, so the gap may narrow if oil revenue funds public services.", "GNI per capita overstates what households receive: oil income flows to the state and foreign partners rather than evenly to people. Inequality-adjusted data would be needed to judge distribution.", "Sri Lanka shows that public investment in health and education can deliver high human development on modest income. But its 2022 debt crisis shows strong social indicators don't prevent economic failure.", "Resource-curse risk: very fast oil-led growth can bring Dutch disease and governance strain. A wealth fund is only as good as the rules on its withdrawals."], "rating": {"relevance": 5, "data": 5, "recency": 5, "evaluation": 5}, "verdict": "Excellent for 4.8. The figures come straight from the UNDP tables, and the comparison is striking and easy to remember. Weakness: the HDI data are for 2023, so they lag the oil boom.", "sources": ["https://hdr.undp.org/sites/default/files/2025_HDR/HDR25_Statistical_Annex_Tables_1-7.pdf", "https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2026/10/08/pr26330-guyana-article-iv"], "mem": {"tag": "Same HDI, 4× income", "story": "Guyana strikes oil (2019) → income per head soars → but its HDI equals much poorer Sri Lanka's (0.776)", "numbers": [["0.776", "HDI of both Guyana and Sri Lanka"], ["$46,959", "Guyana's GNI per capita (PPP)"], ["−54", "Guyana's GNI rank minus HDI rank, the worst gap in the world"]], "use": "GNI vs HDI, composite vs single indicators", "but": "Health and schooling take decades to catch up with sudden oil income."}}, {"id": "india-big-economy-poor-people", "title": "Third biggest by PPP, 153rd per head: India's measurement puzzle", "region": "India", "years": "2011–2026", "subtopic": "4.8", "concepts": ["GDP vs GDP per capita", "nominal vs PPP", "exchange rate effects on measurement", "GDP rebasing", "HDI", "IHDI", "GII", "poverty lines"], "hook": "In April 2026 India 'fell' from 4th to 6th largest economy without producing any less. A weaker rupee and a statistical revision did it.", "facts": ["IMF, April 2026, nominal GDP: Japan $4.38tn, UK $4.26tn, India $4.15tn (6th). In PPP terms India is about $18.9tn, 3rd in the world after China and the US.", "Per head, India is about $2,813 nominal (153rd of 195) and $12,801 in PPP terms (127th).", "The ranking fell because the rupee lost about 11% against the dollar in FY26, and because moving the GDP base year to 2022–23 cut measured nominal GDP by about 3–4%.", "Human Development Report 2025: HDI 0.685 (rank 130). Inequality cuts it by 30.7% (IHDI 0.475). Female labour force participation is 35.1% against 76.4% for men.", "World Bank (2025): extreme poverty at $3.00 a day fell from 27.1% in 2011–12 to 5.3% in 2022–23, from about 344m to 75m people."], "diagram": "None standard. A Lorenz curve can show the inequality loss in the IHDI", "evaluation": ["Nominal rankings at market exchange rates swing with the currency. PPP better reflects living standards but depends on price-survey assumptions, so neither measure is the 'true' one.", "Total GDP measures economic weight, not welfare. Per-head and HDI data show India is still lower-middle income for its citizens, and the IHDI loss and gender gaps show averages hide a lot.", "Poverty headlines depend on the line chosen. At the $4.20 line, poverty was 23.9% in 2022–23, far above the 5.3% extreme figure.", "Revisions show GDP data are not fixed. Growth of over 7% is real, but international comparisons of GDP levels are fragile."], "rating": {"relevance": 5, "data": 5, "recency": 5, "evaluation": 5}, "verdict": "Top-tier 4.8 example that shows every single-indicator pitfall in one country. Weakness: the per-head ranks come from a secondary summary of IMF data.", "sources": ["https://www.republicworld.com/business/india-slips-to-sixth-largest-economy-imf-2026-report-analysis", "https://statisticstimes.com/economy/country/india-gdp-per-capita.php", "https://hdr.undp.org/sites/default/files/2025_HDR/HDR25_Statistical_Annex_Tables_1-7.pdf"], "mem": {"tag": "Big economy, poor people", "story": "Rupee falls + GDP data revised → India drops from 4th to 6th largest → yet 3rd by PPP and 153rd per person", "numbers": [["6th", "India's rank by nominal GDP (IMF, April 2026)"], ["153rd", "India's rank by GDP per person"], ["30.7%", "share of India's HDI lost to inequality (IHDI)"]], "use": "nominal vs PPP, per capita measures, IHDI", "but": "Poverty is 5.3% at $3 a day but 23.9% at $4.20: the line you choose changes the story."}}, {"id": "uk-aid-cut-0-3-percent", "title": "Guns over aid: the UK's cut to 0.3% of GNI", "region": "United Kingdom / Africa and Middle East recipients", "years": "2020–2029", "subtopic": "4.10", "concepts": ["official development assistance", "0.7% UN target", "opportunity cost (defence vs aid)", "bilateral vs multilateral aid", "aid spent at home on refugees", "aid targeting"], "hook": "To pay for defence, the UK is cutting aid from 0.5% to 0.3% of GNI by 2027, its lowest level since the late 1990s, and aid to Africa falls by more than half.", "facts": ["February 2025: the government announced aid would fall from 0.5% to 0.3% of GNI in 2027 to fund higher defence spending: about £9.2bn in 2027, against £15.3bn in 2023.", "2025: UK aid was £13.04bn (0.43% of GNI), down 7.4%. About £2.4bn (18%) was spent on refugee costs inside the UK.", "March 2026 allocations: direct aid to Africa falls 40% in 2026/27 and is 56% below 2024/25 by 2028/29. Aid to the Middle East and North Africa falls 45%.", "Sudan, Palestine and Ukraine are protected. Funding for the Pandemic Fund and polio eradication ends. One think tank estimates 'real' overseas aid could fall to about 0.17% of GNI once refugee costs at home are removed."], "diagram": "None standard. A PPC for defence vs aid shows the opportunity cost", "evaluation": ["Opportunity cost: the government frames this as a security trade-off. Critics say aid is a small share of public spending and the cut reduces UK soft power.", "Quality vs quantity: focusing on fragile and conflict-affected states may raise the impact per pound, but stable low-income countries such as Malawi lose programmes that were working.", "Counting rules: refugee costs at home count as aid, so headline percentages overstate the money reaching developing countries. A useful measurement point.", "The UK cut comes at the same time as cuts by other big donors such as Germany and France. Recipients cannot easily find other donors, so the effects add up."], "rating": {"relevance": 5, "data": 5, "recency": 5, "evaluation": 4}, "verdict": "Strong, recent and well-quantified example on aid levels and targeting. Weakness: effects on recipients are not yet measurable because most cuts bite in 2026–28.", "sources": ["https://www.cgdev.org/blog/breaking-down-prime-minister-starmers-aid-cut", "https://www.bond.org.uk/news/2026/03/oda-allocations-for-2026-onwards-show-the-grim-reality-of-aid-cuts/", "https://www.civilserviceworld.com/news/article/overseas-aid-figures-show-a-provisional-74-drop-in-2025-fcdo"], "mem": {"tag": "Guns over aid", "story": "Defence spending up → UK aid cut from 0.5% to 0.3% of GNI by 2027 → aid to Africa down 56% → refugee costs at home still count as aid", "numbers": [["0.3%", "UK aid as a share of GNI from 2027"], ["0.7%", "UN aid target as a share of GNI"], ["56%", "cut in UK bilateral aid to Africa by 2028/29"]], "use": "foreign aid, opportunity cost", "but": "Focusing on fewer, fragile countries may raise the impact of each pound."}}, {"id": "zambia-common-framework", "title": "Zambia: the slow road to debt relief", "region": "Zambia (also Ghana, Ethiopia)", "years": "2020–2026", "subtopic": "4.9, 4.10", "concepts": ["unsustainable debt", "opportunity cost of debt servicing", "debt restructuring / relief", "G20 Common Framework", "IMF conditionality", "commodity dependence"], "hook": "Zambia defaulted in November 2020 and needed about three and a half years to agree a restructuring. Its debt has since fallen from 133% of GDP to a projected 78%.", "facts": ["Zambia was the first African country to default in the pandemic era (November 2020). It took 28 months to get an IMF programme (August 2022) and another 18 months to agree terms with government creditors.", "IMF (January 2026): public debt fell from 133.4% of GDP (2023) to 101.8% (2024), and is projected at 78.3% for 2026, but the country is still at 'high risk' of debt distress.", "Copper output reached about 890,000 tonnes in 2025 (+8%), short of the 1 million tonne target. Zambia is negotiating a new IMF programme.", "Ghana completed its $3bn IMF programme in 2026, with debt at about 45% of GDP and growth of about 6% in 2025. Ethiopia reached a revised deal with its bondholders in 2026."], "diagram": "None standard. The poverty cycle shows debt service crowding out investment in people; AD/AS for fiscal austerity", "evaluation": ["Restructuring works but slowly. Disputes between China (a large lender) and private bondholders meant years of lost investment. Ghana moved faster partly because Zambia set precedents.", "Restructuring is not the same as relief. The deal cut the debt only modestly, and clauses raise repayments if the economy recovers. Critics say it offers rescheduling rather than the real write-offs of the 2000s HIPC scheme.", "IMF conditions (subsidy cuts, spending cuts) restore stability but can hurt the poor in the short run, a classic equity vs efficiency trade-off.", "The root cause, dependence on copper with volatile prices, remains. Debt relief without diversification risks another crisis."], "rating": {"relevance": 5, "data": 5, "recency": 5, "evaluation": 5}, "verdict": "The go-to debt example, with an IMF-sourced debt path and a clear story. Weakness: complexity, so keep the timeline straight.", "sources": ["https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2026/01/27/pr-26024-zambia-imf-executive-board-completes-sixth-review-under-the-extended-credit-facility", "https://www.hks.harvard.edu/centers/mrcbg/publications/zambia-case-study-sovereign-debt-restructuring-under-g20-common"], "mem": {"tag": "Zambia's debt cut", "story": "Copper-dependent Zambia defaults (2020) → 3.5 years of creditor talks → debt falls from 133% to a projected 78% of GDP", "numbers": [["133%", "Zambia's public debt as % of GDP, 2023"], ["78%", "projected debt as % of GDP, 2026"], ["2020", "year Zambia defaulted"]], "use": "unsustainable debt, debt relief, IMF conditionality", "but": "It's mostly rescheduling rather than write-offs, and copper dependence remains."}}, {"id": "botswana-diamonds-institutions", "title": "Botswana: beating the resource curse, until diamonds lost their shine", "region": "Botswana", "years": "1966–2026", "subtopic": "4.9, 4.10", "concepts": ["institutions and good governance", "resource curse", "over-specialisation on primary commodities", "volatile commodity prices", "fiscal buffers", "economic diversification"], "hook": "Botswana was the textbook proof that good institutions beat the resource curse. Then lab-grown diamonds helped shrink its economy two years in a row.", "facts": ["Classic case: since independence in 1966, Botswana used a 50/50 joint venture with De Beers and careful fiscal rules to become one of the world's fastest-growing economies. HDI 0.731 (rank 111), GNI per capita $16,984 (PPP).", "Diamonds usually provide about a third of government revenue and roughly 70–80% of exports.", "Slump: GDP fell 2.8% in 2024 and 0.7% in 2025. Lab-grown stones are now about 20% of global diamond sales, up from 1% a decade ago. Growth bounced back to 8.7% in Q2 2026 as mines restarted.", "The government's savings fund fell from a P37.2bn peak (2014) to P2.91bn, and the government asked to raise its debt ceiling from 40% to 60% of GDP.", "A February 2025 deal with De Beers extended mining licences to 2054 and raises Botswana's share of output from 30% to 50%."], "diagram": "Diamond market: lab-grown substitutes cut demand for natural diamonds; AD/AS showing a negative AD shock from falling export revenue", "evaluation": ["Institutions matter: Botswana avoided the conflict and corruption seen in other diamond economies (e.g. Sierra Leone), which supports the institutional view of development.", "Good institutions did not fix over-specialisation. Fifty years of diversification plans left the economy exposed to one product, and a permanent shock (synthetic diamonds) is harder to wait out than a cyclical one.", "Inequality: the IHDI shows a 30.4% loss, so resource wealth turned into human development only partly.", "Transferability: Botswana's small population, early leadership and traditional institutions are hard to copy."], "rating": {"relevance": 5, "data": 5, "recency": 5, "evaluation": 5}, "verdict": "Combines a classic examiners already reward with a 2024–26 twist, which makes balanced evaluation easy. Weakness: 2025 GDP and deficit figures have been revised several times.", "sources": ["https://www.businessday.co.za/world/africa/2026-03-31-mining-slump-a-major-reason-for-botswanas-shrinking-economy/", "https://www.polity.org.za/article/botswana-sharply-reduces-budget-deficit-forecast-on-revenue-boost-from-central-bank-2026-09-22", "https://www.mining.com/de-beers-seals-sales-and-mining-contract-with-botswana/"], "mem": {"tag": "Diamonds and institutions", "story": "Good institutions + a diamond deal with De Beers → decades of fast growth → lab-grown diamonds → economy shrinks in 2024 and 2025", "numbers": [["2.8%", "fall in Botswana's GDP in 2024"], ["20%", "lab-grown share of world diamond sales (1% a decade ago)"], ["0.731", "Botswana's HDI"]], "use": "institutions, resource curse, over-specialisation", "but": "Good governance didn't fix depending on one product."}}, {"id": "bangladesh-garments-ldc-graduation", "title": "Bangladesh: stitched out of poverty", "region": "Bangladesh", "years": "1980s–2026", "subtopic": "4.10, 4.9", "concepts": ["export-led growth", "comparative advantage in labour-intensive manufacturing", "women's empowerment through jobs", "preferential market access", "over-specialisation", "LDC graduation", "working conditions"], "hook": "Garments earn about 80% of Bangladesh's exports and employ about 4 million mostly female workers. Now it fears losing duty-free access when it leaves the UN's least-developed list.", "facts": ["FY2025–26: garment exports were $38.7bn, about 80% of total exports of $48bn. The EU took 49%.", "HDI 0.685 (rank 130, equal to India) on a lower GNI per capita of $8,498 (PPP). HDI rose from 0.397 in 1990, the 7th-fastest rise of 125 countries.", "Graduation from least-developed country status is due on 24 November 2026. Bangladesh asked for a 3-year delay, and the request went to the UN General Assembly in July 2026, with a decision pending as of late October.", "Estimates of export losses from losing duty-free EU access range from 6–14% to as high as 32% of total exports.", "The 2013 Rana Plaza factory collapse killed more than 1,100 workers."], "diagram": "PPC and comparative advantage showing gains from specialisation; tariff diagram showing the effect of losing preferential access", "evaluation": ["Export-led growth worked here, raising incomes and women's labour force participation, with knock-on effects on girls' schooling and marriage age. One of the clearest cases of manufacturing-led poverty reduction.", "Over-specialisation: one sector that depends on preferential access and low wages is fragile. Losing duty-free status, automation or tariffs could reverse gains, so diversification (pharmaceuticals, electronics) matters.", "Race to the bottom: Rana Plaza shows the costs of weak regulation. Better standards raise costs but may keep foreign buyers.", "Political risk after the 2024 uprising and 2026 election matters for buyers. Growth may overstate gains if domestic value added stays low."], "rating": {"relevance": 5, "data": 5, "recency": 5, "evaluation": 5}, "verdict": "A classic export-led example made current by the November 2026 graduation decision. Weakness: the outcome is unresolved, so check it before the exam.", "sources": ["https://unb.com.bd/category/Business/eu-share-erodes-as-bangladesh-rmg-exports-slip-164-to-3870-billion-in-fy26/190081", "https://www.thedailystar.net/business/news/un-body-refers-bangladeshs-3-year-ldc-extension-request-general-assembly-4229811"], "mem": {"tag": "Garment-led growth", "story": "Cheap labour → garment factories → 4 million mostly female jobs → garments are 80% of exports → HDI up from 0.397 to 0.685", "numbers": [["80%", "share of Bangladesh's exports that are garments"], ["$38.7bn", "garment exports, FY2025–26"], ["0.685", "Bangladesh's HDI (0.397 in 1990)"]], "use": "export-led growth, women's empowerment, over-specialisation", "but": "One sector, low wages and the Rana Plaza collapse (1,100+ deaths) show the risks."}}, {"id": "vietnam-export-led-umic", "title": "Vietnam: from Doi Moi to upper-middle income", "region": "Vietnam", "years": "1986–2026", "subtopic": "4.10", "concepts": ["export-led growth", "FDI and MNCs", "market-oriented reforms", "trade liberalisation", "income classification", "middle-income trap"], "hook": "Vietnam's GDP per head went from under $500 in 1986 to over $5,000 in 2025, and in July 2026 the World Bank moved it to upper-middle income.", "facts": ["The Doi Moi reforms (1986) opened the economy. GDP per head rose from under $500 to $5,066 in 2025. Trade is nearly 190% of GDP.", "2025: growth of about 8%, exports up about 17% to $475bn, and FDI of $27.6bn, a five-year high.", "The World Bank reclassified Vietnam as upper-middle income on 1 July 2026. GNI per head rose from $4,490 (2024) to $4,970 (2025), above the $4,636 threshold.", "Poverty at $4.20 a day fell from 14% (2010) to under 4% (2025). Electricity access rose from 14% of households (1993) to almost 100%. HDI 0.766 (rank 93)."], "diagram": "AD/AS with AD rising from exports and LRAS shifting right from FDI capital; PPC shifting outward", "evaluation": ["FDI-led manufacturing (Samsung, electronics) brought jobs and technology, but foreign firms dominate exports, so domestic value added is thinner than headline exports suggest.", "Trade at about 190% of GDP means heavy dependence on outside demand, and big trade surpluses invite tariffs from trading partners.", "The income classification is a single indicator. Vietnam crossed the line partly because of population revisions and exchange rates, so the 'promotion' says little about welfare on its own.", "Hard to copy: a one-party state with strong schooling and a location next to Asian supply chains. Reaching high income by 2045 needs about 6% growth for two decades (middle-income trap risk)."], "rating": {"relevance": 5, "data": 5, "recency": 5, "evaluation": 4}, "verdict": "The best current export-led and FDI success story, with a fresh 2026 milestone. Weakness: the story is very positive, so you have to bring the counter-arguments yourself.", "sources": ["https://www.worldbank.org/en/country/vietnam/overview", "https://www.vietnam-briefing.com/news/vietnam-reaches-upper-middle-income-status-under-world-bank-classification.html"], "mem": {"tag": "Doi Moi to middle income", "story": "1986 reforms open the economy → foreign-owned factories arrive → GDP per head rises from $500 to $5,066 → upper-middle income (2026)", "numbers": [["1986", "year of the Doi Moi reforms"], ["$5,066", "Vietnam's GDP per head in 2025"], ["4%", "poverty at $4.20 a day in 2025, down from 14% in 2010"]], "use": "export-led growth, FDI, market-oriented reforms", "but": "Foreign firms dominate exports, and the income 'promotion' rests on a single indicator."}}, {"id": "mpesa-mobile-money", "title": "M-Pesa: banking without banks", "region": "Kenya (and sub-Saharan Africa)", "years": "2007–2026", "subtopic": "4.10 (also 4.9)", "concepts": ["financial inclusion", "mobile money", "access to credit and savings", "women's empowerment", "informal economy", "technology leapfrogging", "private-sector-led development"], "hook": "A study in Science found that M-Pesa lifted 194,000 Kenyan households (2%) out of poverty. Sub-Saharan Africa now has more mobile-money accounts than bank accounts.", "facts": ["Suri and Jack (Science, 2016): access to M-Pesa lifted about 194,000 households (2%) out of poverty, and about 185,000 women moved from farming into business.", "Safaricom FY2026: M-Pesa revenue was KSh 182.7bn (+13.4%), with 43.7 million active users.", "World Bank Findex 2025: 58% of adults in sub-Saharan Africa have an account (49% in 2021), and 40% have a mobile money account, more than the 38% with a bank account.", "1.3 billion adults worldwide are still unbanked, but about 900 million of them own a mobile phone."], "diagram": "None standard. The poverty cycle, broken by access to savings and credit", "evaluation": ["A strong evidence base from a peer-reviewed long-run study, though some researchers challenge the causal estimate. Gains may come from better risk-sharing and remittances rather than productive investment.", "Private monopoly: Safaricom earns large profits and dominates the market. Fees and high-interest digital loans can harm poorer users, which raises regulation questions.", "Hard to copy: it needed light-touch regulation, one dominant telecom firm and dense agent networks. Copies elsewhere (e.g. South Africa) did less well.", "It removes one barrier (no access to finance) but doesn't create jobs or infrastructure, so it works alongside other policies."], "rating": {"relevance": 5, "data": 5, "recency": 4, "evaluation": 5}, "verdict": "Reliable and examiner-friendly, with hard evidence and fresh 2026 numbers. Weakness: the headline poverty study dates from 2016 and its causal claim has critics.", "sources": ["https://www.povertyactionlab.org/sites/default/files/Humanosphere-Tavneet%20Suri%202016.12.15.pdf", "https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2025/07/16/mobile-phone-technology-powers-saving-surge-in-developing-economies"], "mem": {"tag": "Phone banking", "story": "Money sent by text message (2007) → saving and transfers without a bank → 2% of Kenyan households lifted out of poverty", "numbers": [["194,000", "Kenyan households lifted out of poverty"], ["185,000", "women who moved from farming into business"], ["43.7", "million active M-Pesa users"]], "use": "financial inclusion, access to credit, women's empowerment", "but": "Safaricom is a near-monopoly, and copies in other countries did worse."}}, {"id": "grameen-microfinance", "title": "Grameen Bank: microfinance's promise and its reality check", "region": "Bangladesh (and global microfinance)", "years": "1983–2026", "subtopic": "4.10", "concepts": ["microfinance", "women's empowerment", "group lending", "access to credit", "poverty trap", "randomised evaluations"], "hook": "Grameen and Muhammad Yunus won the 2006 Nobel Peace Prize for lending to poor women, but six randomised trials later found microcredit's effects were 'modestly positive, but not transformative'.", "facts": ["Grameen Bank had about 9.5 million borrowers in 2022, 96.8% of them women, reaching about 94% of Bangladesh's villages. The bank reports repayment of about 95%, which some critics question.", "2006 Nobel Peace Prize to Grameen and Yunus 'for their efforts to create economic and social development from below'.", "Banerjee, Karlan and Zinman (2015) reviewed six randomised trials on four continents and found 'modestly positive, but not transformative, effects': more business activity but no significant fall in poverty.", "Yunus led Bangladesh's interim government after the 2024 uprising until the February 2026 election."], "diagram": "Poverty cycle (credit as a way out of the low-savings, low-investment trap)", "evaluation": ["It gives women control of income and status, and group lending solves the problem of borrowers having no collateral. But the trials show average income gains are small.", "Over-indebtedness: high interest rates and multiple loans (e.g. the 2010 Andhra Pradesh crisis in India) can deepen debt traps.", "Credit without markets: tiny businesses often compete in crowded local markets, so microcredit can't replace jobs from industrialisation. Compare the garment sector's bigger impact on women.", "Savings, insurance and mobile money may matter more than credit, which links to M-Pesa."], "rating": {"relevance": 5, "data": 3, "recency": 2, "evaluation": 5}, "verdict": "A classic examiners still reward, especially paired with the trial evidence. Weakness: the operating data are from 2022 and the story is old.", "sources": ["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grameen_Bank", "https://economics.mit.edu/sites/default/files/publications/Six%20Randomized%20Evaluations%20of%20Microcredit.pdf"], "mem": {"tag": "Microcredit for women", "story": "Small loans to groups of poor women → 9.5 million borrowers → Nobel Prize 2006 → trials find effects 'modest, not transformative'", "numbers": [["9.5", "million Grameen borrowers"], ["96.8%", "share of Grameen borrowers who are women"], ["2006", "year Grameen won the Nobel Peace Prize"]], "use": "microfinance, women's empowerment, poverty trap", "but": "Six randomised trials found more business activity but no clear fall in poverty."}}, {"id": "india-digital-public-infrastructure", "title": "India Stack: development through digital public infrastructure", "region": "India", "years": "2009–2026", "subtopic": "4.10 (also 4.9)", "concepts": ["digital infrastructure", "institutional reform", "reducing corruption and leakage", "financial inclusion", "government provision of public goods", "direct benefit transfers"], "hook": "India's UPI payment system handled about 228 billion transactions in 2025, and the government claims direct cash transfers saved ₹3.48 lakh crore by cutting out middlemen.", "facts": ["The 'JAM trinity': Jan Dhan bank accounts, Aadhaar biometric ID (from 2009) and mobile phones. The World Bank (2023) said India reached 80% financial inclusion in about six years, up from about 25% in 2008.", "UPI in 2025: about 228 billion transactions worth nearly ₹300 lakh crore, up 32.5% by volume on 2024.", "Direct Benefit Transfer: the government reports savings of ₹3.48 lakh crore from 2009 to 2024 by removing fake and duplicate claimants.", "Extreme poverty fell from 27.1% (2011–12) to 5.3% (2022–23), but India's HDI rank is still 130."], "diagram": "None standard. Reduced leakage can be shown as a cheaper subsidy, or LRAS shifting right from better institutions", "evaluation": ["The state built open platforms and private firms (PhonePe, Google Pay) built on top, a mix of market-oriented and interventionist strategies.", "The savings figure comes from the government, so treat it with caution. Independent checks are limited.", "Exclusion errors: ID failures have reportedly denied food rations to some legitimate claimants. Privacy concerns exist, and many bank accounts lie dormant.", "India is exporting the model, but it needed huge scale and near-universal ID and mobile coverage. Smaller or poorer states face high set-up costs."], "rating": {"relevance": 4, "data": 4, "recency": 5, "evaluation": 4}, "verdict": "A modern, distinctive 4.10 example of institutional and infrastructure reform. Weakness: key claims come from government sources, and the link to poverty reduction is indirect.", "sources": ["https://www.businesstoday.in/technology/news/story/upi-ends-2025-on-a-high-with-record-monthly-and-annual-transactions-with-plans-for-future-initiatives-509218-2026-01-02", "https://www.deccanherald.com/business/economy/india-has-used-digital-public-infrastructure-to-achieve-80-financial-inclusion-in-6-years-world-bank-2678859"], "mem": {"tag": "India Stack", "story": "Biometric ID + bank accounts + phones → cash sent straight to people → fake claimants removed → instant payments explode", "numbers": [["228", "billion UPI payments made in 2025"], ["80%", "Indian adults with an account, reached in about 6 years"], ["₹3.48", "lakh crore saved through direct transfers (government claim)"]], "use": "infrastructure, institutional reform, reducing corruption", "but": "The savings figure comes from the government, and ID failures have shut some people out."}}, {"id": "sudan-war-development-reversal", "title": "Sudan: how war erases development", "region": "Sudan", "years": "2023–2026", "subtopic": "4.9", "concepts": ["conflict as a barrier", "destruction of physical and human capital", "displacement", "famine", "capital flight", "humanitarian aid shortfalls"], "hook": "Since the war began in April 2023, Sudan's economy has shrunk by an estimated 29% and then a further 14%, and about two in five people face crisis-level hunger.", "facts": ["War between the army and the Rapid Support Forces began on 15 April 2023. World Bank estimates put GDP falls at 29.4% (2023) and 14% (2024). Other estimates differ, so quote a range.", "May 2026: nearly 19.5 million people, about 2 in 5, face crisis-level food insecurity. Famine was confirmed in El Fasher and Kadugli in 2025.", "Close to 9 million people were internally displaced as of March 2026.", "HDI 0.511 (rank 176), already falling before the war. Unemployment rose from 32% (2022) to 47% (2024), and the Sudanese pound lost more than half its value."], "diagram": "AD/AS with SRAS and LRAS shifting left (destroyed capital); PPC shifting inward; poverty cycle", "evaluation": ["Conflict is the most powerful barrier because it destroys everything at once: physical capital, human capital (schools and hospitals) and institutions. Growth strategies are irrelevant until there is peace.", "Conflict trap: low income raises the risk of conflict, and conflict lowers income. Gold revenue finances armed groups, which links to the resource curse.", "Humanitarian aid saves lives but cannot rebuild an economy during war, and the 2026 appeal is badly underfunded amid global aid cuts.", "War makes statistics very uncertain (estimates differ by over 10 percentage points), so quote ranges."], "rating": {"relevance": 5, "data": 4, "recency": 5, "evaluation": 4}, "verdict": "The clearest current case of conflict as a barrier, with official UN numbers. Weakness: GDP estimates vary widely, and it suits explaining a barrier better than debating policy.", "sources": ["https://www.unicef.org.uk/press-releases/risk-of-famine-persists-as-nearly-19-5-million-people-face-acute-food-insecurity-in-sudan/", "https://hdr.undp.org/sites/default/files/2025_HDR/HDR25_Statistical_Annex_Tables_1-7.pdf"], "mem": {"tag": "War erases development", "story": "War starts April 2023 → GDP falls 29%, then 14% → 9 million displaced → famine", "numbers": [["29%", "fall in Sudan's GDP in 2023"], ["9", "million people displaced inside Sudan"], ["19.5", "million people facing crisis-level hunger, 2026"]], "use": "conflict as a barrier, human and physical capital", "but": "War makes data so unreliable that GDP estimates differ by 10+ points."}}, {"id": "afghanistan-women-exclusion", "title": "Afghanistan: the price of excluding half the population", "region": "Afghanistan", "years": "2021–2026", "subtopic": "4.9, 4.8", "concepts": ["gender inequality as a barrier", "human capital", "Gender Inequality Index", "Gender Development Index", "labour force participation", "opportunity cost of exclusion"], "hook": "Since the Taliban barred girls from school after grade six in 2021, more than 2.6 million girls have been denied secondary education, at a cost the UNDP puts at $920m, about 5.8% of GDP.", "facts": ["Girls have been barred from school beyond grade six since September 2021 and women from universities since December 2022. More than 2.6 million girls have been denied secondary education (UNICEF, 2026).", "UNDP (2025): excluding women costs about $920m (about 5.8% of GDP) over 2024–26. Only about 7% of women are employed, down from about 15% before 2021.", "Afghanistan's Gender Development Index (0.660) is the second-lowest in the world. Women average 1.2 years of schooling against 3.9 for men, and estimated income per head is $721 for women against $3,198 for men.", "UNICEF projects a loss of up to 20,000 women teachers and 5,400 female health workers by 2030, because no new women are being trained."], "diagram": "PPC with the economy inside the frontier (unused female labour); poverty cycle with low human capital", "evaluation": ["Clear evidence that gender inequality lowers output. The economy runs inside its PPC, and the loss compounds over generations through fewer female teachers and health workers.", "The UNDP and UNICEF cost estimates use different methods. Both are probably underestimates because they miss effects on the next generation's health and education.", "Policy dilemma for donors: tying aid to women's rights may push the regime further away, while unconditional aid may entrench it.", "Contrast with Bangladesh, where women's paid work raised development. That lets you argue women's empowerment is a cause, not just a result, of development."], "rating": {"relevance": 5, "data": 5, "recency": 5, "evaluation": 4}, "verdict": "Powerful, well-sourced example of gender inequality as a barrier that links to the GII and GDI. Weakness: it is an extreme case driven by ideology, so use India's 35% female labour force participation for everyday gender gaps.", "sources": ["https://www.unicef.org/press-releases/more-26-million-girls-afghanistan-have-been-denied-secondary-education-2021", "https://hdr.undp.org/sites/default/files/2025_HDR/HDR25_Statistical_Annex_Tables_1-7.pdf"], "mem": {"tag": "Girls barred from school", "story": "Taliban bar girls after grade six (2021) → 2.6 million girls out of school → only 7% of women employed → cost about 5.8% of GDP", "numbers": [["2.6", "million girls denied secondary school"], ["7%", "share of Afghan women in work"], ["5.8%", "GDP cost of excluding women, 2024–26"]], "use": "gender inequality as a barrier, GII and GDI", "but": "It's an extreme case driven by ideology: use India's 35% female participation for everyday gender gaps."}}, {"id": "chile-cherries-china", "title": "Chile's cherries for Chinese New Year", "region": "Chile and China", "years": "2017–2026", "subtopic": "4.1, 4.4", "concepts": ["comparative advantage", "specialisation", "free trade agreement", "export revenue", "more choice for consumers", "lower prices"], "hook": "Chile's cherries ripen in the southern summer, exactly when China wants red fruit for Chinese New Year, so Chile now sells China over $3bn of cherries a season.", "facts": ["In the 2024/25 season (to May) Chile exported a record 625,208 tonnes of cherries, up 51.1%, worth $3.37bn, up 37.8%.", "China took about 90.8% of the volume and $3.08bn of the value. Chile's cherry exports to China grew about 29% a year on average up to 2024.", "Chile's comparative advantage: central Chile has the cold winter chill hours cherries need, and its harvest lands in China's winter, when Northern Hemisphere orchards are out of season.", "The 2017 upgrade of the China–Chile FTA put more than 97% of traded products, including cherries, under zero tariffs. Cherry planting rose from about 38,392 ha (2019) to 70,686 ha (2024).", "Consumers gained: in January 2026 Chengdu supermarkets promoted 2.5 kg boxes at about $14.1, roughly 40% cheaper than a year earlier."], "diagram": "Draw the PPC/comparative advantage diagram with Chile (cherries) and China (manufactured goods), and show both consuming outside their own PPC after trade. For HL, add the import diagram for China's cherry market: removing the tariff lowers the price from Pw + t to Pw, raising consumer surplus and imports.", "evaluation": ["Short run vs long run: the 2024/25 record volume pushed prices down sharply. Export value rose 37.8% but volume rose 51.1%, so value per kg fell from about $5.9 to $5.4. Growers who planted for high prices saw lower profits.", "Stakeholders: Chinese consumers gain cheaper winter fruit and Chilean farm workers gain jobs, but Chinese cherry growers face more competition and Chilean growers carry the price risk.", "Limits of comparative advantage: the theory assumes no transport costs and stable demand. Cherries must cross the Pacific in about 23 days by sea, and one holiday-driven market sets the price.", "Priorities: about 90% of sales to one buyer is a dependency risk (link to the rare-earths example). Diversifying to Korea, Taiwan and India is growing fast but from a tiny base."], "rating": {"relevance": 5, "data": 5, "recency": 5, "evaluation": 4}, "verdict": "The cleanest 'comparative advantage plus FTA' story for 4.1, with official USDA data and a clear consumer gain. Weakness: it is one product, so it shows the gains of specialisation and the risks of over-specialisation at the same time.", "sources": ["https://chilealimentos.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Stone-Fruit-Annual_Santiago_Chile_CI2025-0017.pdf", "https://www.freshplaza.com/latin-america/article/9800994/chilean-cherry-exports-to-china-spread-beyond-holiday-peak/", "https://www.freshplaza.com/latin-america/article/9806179/chile-sends-90-per-cent-of-cherries-to-china/", "https://www.freshplaza.com/asia/article/9595157/chile-s-fresh-cherry-exports-to-china-hit-record-high/", "https://www.freshfruitportal.com/news/2025/03/19/final-stretch-of-chilean-cherry-season-sees-51-rise-in-exports/"], "mem": {"tag": "Chinese New Year cherries", "story": "Chile's harvest lands in China's winter → FTA makes cherries tariff-free → Chile plants nearly double the orchards → record $3.37bn season, 91% to China → prices fall for consumers", "numbers": [["$3.37bn", "Chile's cherry exports in 2024/25, up 37.8%"], ["90.8%", "share of Chile's cherry volume sold to China"], ["97%", "share of traded products at zero tariff under the 2017 FTA upgrade"]], "use": "comparative advantage, specialisation, free trade agreements, lower prices, more choice", "but": "Record volumes crashed prices, and selling 90% to one country leaves Chile exposed to a single market."}}, {"id": "morocco-car-exports", "title": "Morocco: Africa's car factory for Europe", "region": "Morocco and Europe", "years": "2012–2026", "subtopic": "4.1, 4.10", "concepts": ["economies of scale", "foreign direct investment", "access to larger markets", "export revenue", "technology transfer", "export-led growth"], "hook": "Renault and Stellantis build cars in Morocco to sell in Europe, and cars are now Morocco's biggest export, about a third of all goods it sells abroad.", "facts": ["Automotive exports were MAD 159.2bn in 2025, out of total exports of MAD 470bn, so about 34%. It was the top export sector for a third year running, ahead of phosphates at MAD 99.7bn.", "In 2024 Renault built 413,600 cars at Casablanca and Tangier, and Stellantis built 175,000 at Kenitra. Renault says 80% of its Moroccan output goes to Europe.", "Renault Tangier opened in 2012, employs 7,000 people and is now Renault's largest plant in the world. The Moroccan-made Dacia Sandero was Europe's best-selling car in 2024.", "In 2025 Stellantis invested €1.2bn to double Kenitra's capacity, with €702m for local suppliers and 3,100 new direct jobs. Local content is 65% at Renault, and Stellantis targets 75% by 2030.", "After a 2025 dip (vehicle building fell 13.5%), car exports rebounded 17.4% to MAD 93.65bn in the first half of 2026."], "diagram": "Draw a long-run average cost (LRAC) curve: selling to the large EU market lets plants produce at high output and lower average cost (economies of scale). Pair it with AD/AS: rising exports (X in AD) and FDI shift AD right, and capital investment shifts LRAS right.", "evaluation": ["Short run vs long run: the 2025 dip shows dependence on European demand and on a few model cycles. In the long run, rising local content (65% to 75%) means more value is added in Morocco.", "Stakeholders: Moroccan workers and local suppliers gain jobs and skills, and EU buyers get cheaper cars. Foreign owners keep much of the profit, and repatriated profits leave Morocco.", "Limits of comparative advantage: Morocco's edge is partly made by policy (investment incentives, EU market access, port investment), not just natural endowments, so it could shrink if wages rise or incentives end.", "Priorities: Morocco's trade deficit still widened 17.7% to MAD 259.1bn in the first nine months of 2025, so cars help export revenue but do not solve the current account on their own. Is diversifying into aerospace and EVs a better priority than more car assembly?"], "rating": {"relevance": 5, "data": 4, "recency": 5, "evaluation": 4}, "verdict": "Best 4.1 example for economies of scale, FDI and access to a bigger market, and a strong 4.10 export-led growth case. Weakness: figures are in dirhams, and the gains depend heavily on two foreign firms and European demand.", "sources": ["https://fr.hespress.com/492427-exportations-lautomobile-en-tete-au-maroc-avec-1592-mmdh-en-2025.html", "https://ecoactu.ma/exportations-du-maroc-lautomobile-reste-lindetronable/", "https://www.maroc.ma/index%2Ephp/en/news/morocco-powers-europes-best-selling-cars", "https://www.moroccoworldnews.com/2025/07/230333/stellantis-strengthens-presence-in-morocco-with-e1-2-billion-project/", "https://www.moroccoworldnews.com/2025/11/269529/morocco-records-2-7-decline-in-automotive-exports-in-2025/", "https://fnh.ma/article/actualites-marocaines/automobile-maroc-exportations-93-milliards-dirhams-juin-2026"], "mem": {"tag": "Moroccan Sandero", "story": "Renault opens Tangier plant in 2012 → cars built at scale for Europe → Stellantis adds €1.2bn at Kenitra → cars become a third of exports → local suppliers grow", "numbers": [["MAD 159.2bn", "Morocco's car exports in 2025, about 34% of all exports"], ["80%", "share of Renault's Moroccan output sold to Europe"], ["65%", "local content in Renault's Moroccan cars"]], "use": "economies of scale, FDI, access to larger markets, export revenue, technology transfer", "but": "The industry depends on two foreign firms and European demand, and much of the profit goes abroad."}}, {"id": "new-zealand-china-fta-dairy", "title": "New Zealand: the FTA that built a dairy giant", "region": "New Zealand and China", "years": "2008–2025", "subtopic": "4.1, 4.4", "concepts": ["free trade agreement", "comparative advantage", "access to larger markets", "export revenue", "specialisation", "trade creation"], "hook": "New Zealand signed a free trade deal with China in 2008, and its exports to China grew about eight times by 2024, led by milk powder.", "facts": ["New Zealand's exports to China rose from NZ$2.5bn in 2008 to NZ$20.85bn in 2024, an average of 14.1% a year, against 7.7% a year to the rest of the world.", "The FTA removed tariffs on over 98% of New Zealand's goods exports to China. China is now its largest trading partner, with two-way trade over NZ$41bn in the year to September 2025.", "The last safeguard duties on milk powder ended on 1 January 2024, so all New Zealand dairy now enters China duty-free, saving about NZ$350m a year in tariffs.", "Dairy exports to China were about NZ$8bn a year in 2021 to 2023, about half of it milk powder. In 2024, 31% of New Zealand's dairy exports went to China.", "Comparative advantage: grass-fed cows on cheap pasture give New Zealand low-cost milk, and the FTA gave it access to the world's biggest dairy import market."], "diagram": "Draw the PPC/comparative advantage diagram (New Zealand: dairy; China: manufactured goods), showing both consuming beyond their PPC after trade. For 4.4, a tariff-removal diagram for China's milk powder market: price falls from Pw + t to Pw, imports rise and Chinese consumer surplus rises.", "evaluation": ["Short run vs long run: tariffs were cut slowly over 16 years with safeguards, giving Chinese farmers time to adjust. The full gain only arrived in 2024.", "Stakeholders: New Zealand farmers and Fonterra gained a huge market, and Chinese families got cheaper, trusted infant formula. Chinese dairy farmers faced tougher competition.", "Limits of the FTA argument: exports also grew because Chinese incomes rose and tastes changed, so not all of the growth is due to the FTA (correlation is not causation).", "Priorities: 20.6% of New Zealand's exports going to China creates dependency. Fonterra says growth in China has slowed, and a diplomatic dispute could hit farmers hard (compare Australia's barley and wine tariffs from China)."], "rating": {"relevance": 5, "data": 4, "recency": 4, "evaluation": 5}, "verdict": "Best long-run 'before and after' trade deal case, perfect for linking 4.1 gains to 4.4 FTAs. Weakness: the big growth story is 2008 to 2019, and the FTA's own effect is hard to separate from rising Chinese incomes.", "sources": ["https://auckland.mofcom.gov.cn/scdy/art/2025/art_9d529a6906754ca3ac7e1423e8eeb090.html", "https://www.mfat.govt.nz/br/trade/free-trade-agreements/free-trade-agreements-in-force/nz-china-free-trade-agreement/overview", "https://dairyreporter.com/Article/2024/01/22/china-s-final-tariff-removals-open-new-opportunities-for-new-zealand-dairy-brands"], "mem": {"tag": "Kiwi milk to China", "story": "2008 FTA signed → tariffs cut on 98% of exports → milk powder floods into China → exports to China grow 14.1% a year → last dairy tariffs gone in 2024", "numbers": [["NZ$2.5bn to NZ$20.85bn", "New Zealand's exports to China, 2008 to 2024"], ["14.1%", "yearly export growth to China, vs 7.7% to the rest of the world"], ["NZ$350m", "yearly tariff savings once dairy became fully duty-free in 2024"]], "use": "free trade agreements, comparative advantage, access to larger markets, export revenue", "but": "Rising Chinese incomes explain part of the growth, and relying so heavily on one buyer is a dependency risk."}}, {"id": "mission-300-africa-electricity", "title": "Mission 300: wiring up Africa (SDG 7)", "region": "Sub-Saharan Africa", "years": "2010–2026", "subtopic": "4.7, 4.10", "concepts": ["SDG 7 affordable and clean energy", "energy poverty", "infrastructure investment", "multilateral development banks", "co-financing and private investment", "measuring SDG progress"], "hook": "Sub-Saharan Africa holds 86% of the world's people without electricity, so the World Bank and African Development Bank promised to connect 300 million Africans by 2030.", "facts": ["In 2024, 563 million people in sub-Saharan Africa had no electricity: 86% of the world total, up from 49% in 2010 (Tracking SDG7 2026).", "The number barely moved: 565 million in 2010 and 563 million in 2024, so new connections only just kept up with population growth.", "Mission 300, launched in 2024, targets 300 million people by 2030: 250 million from the World Bank Group and 50 million from the African Development Bank (AfDB).", "In June 2026 the two banks said over 50 million people in 40 countries had been connected, with nearly $15bn committed and about $4.5bn in co-financing. 30 countries have launched National Energy Compacts.", "Tanzania connected 7.5 million people, a five-fold rise in its yearly pace of electrification."], "diagram": "None standard. A PPC shifting outward shows grid and solar investment adding capital and productive capacity. A positive externality diagram also works: power for clinics and schools has external benefits, so the market under-provides it and subsidised connections move output towards the socially optimal level.", "evaluation": ["Measurement: a BusinessDay critique (June 2026) says the 50 million is mostly relabelled. The World Bank counts connections from any bank project active since July 2023, including ones approved in 2016, so the figure does not prove the mission added anything new.", "Scale: the Tracking SDG7 2026 report says Africa's pace of electrification must more than triple for universal access by 2030. 50 million in two years is about one sixth of the target, with only four years left.", "Who pays: most money is development bank loans, which add to public debt in countries already struggling with debt service (a 4.9 barrier). Higher tariffs that attract private investors can make power unaffordable for the poorest households.", "Short vs long run: a connection is not the same as reliable, affordable power. A small solar kit counts as a connection but may not run a fridge or a workshop, so the growth payoff depends on quality and on building generation, not just counting households."], "rating": {"relevance": 5, "data": 4, "recency": 5, "evaluation": 5}, "verdict": "The best current SDG 7 case: a clear financing mechanism and very recent numbers. Weakness: the headline 50 million is disputed, so always add the additionality critique.", "sources": ["https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2026/06/16/under-mission-300-a-new-way-of-doing-business-connects-over-50-million-people-to-electricity-across-africa", "https://www.worldbank.org/ext/en/energizingafrica/progress-portal", "https://www.ecofinagency.com/news-industry/2506-56796-sub-saharan-africa-holds-86-of-global-electricity-access-gap-sdg-7-report", "https://businessday.ng/opinion/article/the-world-bank-said-mission-300-has-electrified-50-million-africans-so-we-checked/"], "mem": {"tag": "Mission 300 lights", "story": "563m Africans have no power → banks pledge 300m connections by 2030 → nearly $15bn committed → 50m claimed by June 2026 → critics: old projects relabelled", "numbers": [["563m", "people in sub-Saharan Africa without electricity (2024)"], ["86%", "Africa's share of the world's people without power"], ["50m", "people connected under Mission 300 by June 2026 (claimed)"]], "use": "SDG 7, infrastructure investment, multilateral development banks, development finance, measuring progress", "but": "Most of the 50 million came from projects approved before Mission 300 began, so the headline may overstate what it added."}}, {"id": "costa-rica-forest-payments", "title": "Costa Rica: paying landowners to regrow forest", "region": "Costa Rica (Latin America)", "years": "1987–2026", "subtopic": "4.7, 4.10", "concepts": ["SDG 15 life on land", "SDG 7 and SDG 13", "payments for ecosystem services", "positive externalities", "Pigouvian tax", "climate vulnerability"], "hook": "Costa Rica uses part of its fuel tax to pay landowners to protect forest, and forest cover has nearly tripled since 1987.", "facts": ["Forest cover fell to about 21% of the country in 1987 and is now close to 60%.", "Since 1997 the FONAFIFO payments scheme has put more than 1.3 million hectares under contract, invested over $524m and involved more than 18,000 families.", "It is funded by 3.5% of national fuel tax revenue and 25% of water concession fees, so fuel users and water users pay for the services forests provide.", "Electricity is almost all renewable, mostly hydro: renewables covered 97.3% of demand in 2025 and fossil-fuel plants only 1.38%.", "In the 2024 El Niño drought renewables fell to 86.8% of demand and fossil power rose to 10.4%. The state utility ICE announced cuts of up to 3 hours a day from 13 May 2024, the first rationing since 2007."], "diagram": "Positive production externality: forests store carbon, protect water and biodiversity, but landowners are not paid for this, so MSC is below MPC and too little forest is supplied. The payment works like a subsidy that shifts MPC down to the socially optimal quantity. The fuel tax side is a negative externality diagram with a Pigouvian tax.", "evaluation": ["Cause and effect: a November 2025 study (Hernández-Blanco and Costanza) says the payments' impact is hard to separate from the 1996 ban on deforestation, the end of farm subsidies, falling cattle profits and ecotourism. Payments were one cause, not the only one.", "Trade-off between goals: depending on hydropower made SDG 7 vulnerable to drought, so climate change (SDG 13) pushed the country back to fossil power in 2024. The response is about 600 MW of new geothermal, solar and wind by 2030.", "Who pays: all fuel users fund the scheme, including bus riders and poorer drivers, while the money goes to people who own land. The poorest rural workers, who own no land, may gain little.", "Transferability: Costa Rica is small and stable, earns a lot from tourism and has hydro and geothermal resources. Low-income countries with weak institutions or a heavy reliance on farm exports may find it harder to copy."], "rating": {"relevance": 5, "data": 3, "recency": 4, "evaluation": 5}, "verdict": "Use it for SDG 15, externalities and the growth vs environment trade-off: a clear market-based mechanism, plus a drought twist for evaluation. Weakness: the forest figures come from secondary reports and the true effect of the payments is debated.", "sources": ["https://environmentenergyleader.com/stories/what-costa-ricas-forest-recovery-reveals-about-conservation,131727", "https://delfino.cr/2026/02/generacion-electrica-renovable-repunta-en-2025-tras-caida-del-ano-anterior", "https://www.revistaeyn.com/centroamericaymundo/costa-rica-produjo-casi-99-de-su-electricidad-con-fuentes-renovables-en-2025-OE29691087", "http://www.latinnews.com/index.php?option=com_legacyk2&view=item&id=101388"], "mem": {"tag": "Costa Rica pays trees", "story": "Forest cut to 21% by 1987 → 1996 Forest Law: fuel tax pays landowners to protect forest → forest back near 60% → 2024 drought: hydro falls, fossil power hits 10%", "numbers": [["21% to ~60%", "forest cover, 1987 vs today"], ["3.5%", "share of fuel tax revenue that funds forest payments"], ["10.4%", "electricity demand met by fossil fuels in the 2024 drought (1.38% in 2025)"]], "use": "SDG 15, positive externalities, Pigouvian taxes, payments for ecosystem services, sustainability trade-offs", "but": "Forest also came back because of a logging ban, falling cattle profits and tourism, so the payments may get too much credit."}}, {"id": "china-extreme-poverty-declared-over", "title": "China declares extreme poverty over (SDG 1)", "region": "China (East Asia)", "years": "2012–2023", "subtopic": "4.7, 4.8", "concepts": ["SDG 1 no poverty", "absolute poverty", "international poverty lines", "government intervention", "income inequality (Gini)", "measuring progress"], "hook": "In 2021 China said it had ended extreme rural poverty, but on the World Bank line for upper-middle-income countries about 1 in 6 Chinese were still poor.", "facts": ["In February 2021 Xi Jinping said the last 98.99 million rural poor had been lifted out in eight years, and all 832 poor counties and 128,000 poor villages had left the list.", "The state spent nearly 1.6 trillion yuan (about $246bn) over those eight years and relocated more than 9.6 million people out of poor areas since late 2012.", "The official rural line was about 4,000 yuan a year, which the World Bank puts at about $2.30 a day (2017 PPP), just above its $2.15 extreme poverty line.", "World Bank (October 2024): in 2021, 17% of Chinese, about 241 million people, still lived below $6.85 a day, the line typical of upper-middle-income countries. Nearly half of them lived in cities.", "Progress is slowing: about 27 million people rose above $6.85 a day in 2023, down from around 50 million a year in 2018 and 2019."], "diagram": "None standard. Draw a bar chart of China's poverty rate at different lines (0% at $2.15, 17% at $6.85 in 2021) to show that the line chosen changes the answer. A Lorenz curve can add inequality: World Bank consumption Gini 35.7 (2021) vs official income Gini 46.7 (2022).", "evaluation": ["Measurement: whether poverty is 'over' depends on the line. It is 0% at the official and $2.15 lines but 17% at $6.85. The official count only covered rural people, so poor urban migrants were left out, and no new official line has been set since 2021.", "Mechanism and who pays: a top-down campaign with heavy state spending, officials sent to villages, roads and relocation worked fast. It needs a strong state and big fiscal resources, so few low-income countries could copy it.", "Trade-offs: relocating 9.6 million people raises questions about consent and lost livelihoods, and the fast, energy-intensive growth that paid for the campaign came with high emissions, in tension with SDG 13.", "Short vs long run: many families rose only just above a low line, so illness or an economic slowdown could push them back. China kept a five-year transition period to watch for relapse, and inequality stays high (official Gini 46.7 in 2022)."], "rating": {"relevance": 5, "data": 4, "recency": 3, "evaluation": 5}, "verdict": "Use it for SDG 1 and for measurement questions (4.8): one country, two answers depending on the poverty line. Weakness: the core data is from 2020 to 2023 and the official figures cannot be independently checked.", "sources": ["https://regional.chinadaily.com.cn/hainan/2021-02/26/c_596834.htm", "https://www.tribuneindia.com/news/world/president-xi-claims-complete-victory-in-eradicating-absolute-poverty-in-china-217396/amp", "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2021/2/25/chinas-xi-declares-complete-victory-against-rural-poverty", "https://documents1.worldbank.org/curated/en/099656501032511659/text/IDU-18b182ae-5c43-4b6e-90ba-cb7c8c03a9d9.txt", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_against_poverty"], "mem": {"tag": "China's poverty victory", "story": "Official line about $2.30 a day → 1.6tn yuan, officials in villages, roads, relocation → 2021: zero rural poor declared → World Bank: 17% still below $6.85", "numbers": [["98.99m", "rural people lifted out of poverty, 2012–2020 (official)"], ["1.6tn yuan", "state spending on poverty in eight years (about $246bn)"], ["17%", "Chinese below $6.85 a day in 2021 (World Bank)"]], "use": "SDG 1, absolute poverty, poverty lines, measuring development, government intervention", "but": "At the poverty line normal for upper-middle-income countries, about 241 million Chinese were still poor in 2021."}}];
